n⁡a \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1de2e53b-3e5a-515c-aeb7-08b2b37df9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269517} {:answer/id #uuid "2044f8ba-a526-5264-9c8b-279475bc6079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269538} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef60e4f-a72a-5357-acf3-4233e5a280e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-25{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269981} {:answer/id #uuid "d1396c9b-3b30-5a34-9af3-bb9767985973" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-10{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271110} {:answer/id #uuid "e97a70aa-76d8-5b9c-aa63-ecd9752ccf4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+10{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1de2e53b-3e5a-515c-aeb7-08b2b37df9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-5{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269517} :answer-field/id #uuid "501bcbfe-9b64-5b29-8ec4-43efe59a3a60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0a5ade6b-92c1-5055-8b0b-c80cbaff9d2f" :question/math-academy-id "q-140281" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1$ as the sum of a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x+25 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-10x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-10x+25)+1 & = \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}+1 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272045 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf9b812d-0dbf-5a67-9d45-3cc5e373b2b1" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated: Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "098366e9-6bbf-557f-8b0b-c28f1f5c5d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+12{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269356} {:answer/id #uuid "229c01ab-0ea9-5605-90a1-1d5a7751e77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269568} {:answer/id #uuid "9d389434-bcbe-5f2b-ab97-000df772d7d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-12{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270659} {:answer/id #uuid "aa8a2c46-7cb2-59b0-9203-35410fcdd6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-22{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270793} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f56343-9654-51c6-8939-85100071b4dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "229c01ab-0ea9-5605-90a1-1d5a7751e77a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-11{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269568} :answer-field/id #uuid "90eb344c-2847-58a2-8769-e020f945532e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff51e775-e2b4-585e-a683-f90782853abe" :question/math-academy-id "q-140283" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2ax+{a}^{2}=(x-a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-22x+121 & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-22x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 11x+{11}^{2} & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}^{2}-22x+121)-4 & = \\\\ (x-11{)}^{2}-4 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272085 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0884ad38-9d14-5f8e-b665-d8b35b5af237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269346} {:answer/id #uuid "0db0ac08-c4ce-5d3f-845f-d38358413506" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269388} {:answer/id #uuid "2934301c-7684-5c1f-b910-d7819431eba3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269640} {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} {:answer/id #uuid "81639cc6-e903-5cd2-bc14-50bfc72b4d70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7789397b-7630-5bd8-9c80-42e72f128cee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270328} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7c2979c-8072-5209-9ca0-20ccbaf77003" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d2ffa4af-5dbe-569b-8d78-f233584a8913" :question/math-academy-id "q-140431" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}+4{x}^{2}-3) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+4\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+8x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x}+8x)\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot {e}^{1}+8\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2e+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400909 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3559bdb9-c007-500b-83db-63f2d91cd407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400911} {:answer/id #uuid "f1984a87-93e4-523e-80b4-a37c28b7dc5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400912} {:answer/id #uuid "3f15fef9-83c3-50e8-980b-72c67ab596d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400913} {:answer/id #uuid "f52c0001-2df6-56e0-ae3c-2dca7d79a58b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400914} {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d7950f6f-fd87-5dac-ba8c-650fe8a19888" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400915} :answer-field/id #uuid "5806f142-c711-5da8-b1d4-68c8001a3f1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ab70a1a-e04b-524f-9866-e86f88144126" :question/math-academy-id "q-141140" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b362831ba2248e03537551c412ae7537646dcd09f0d00a598905f0d52a258da9.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is given above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=-3.$ Therefore, $x=-3$ is a *simple* root."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272221 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129253 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d48e2a0-1a76-5eec-a98c-4a7eb16f70cb" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Multiplicity of a Root Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7a762869-8c41-5b99-945a-904933273d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a simple root" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270354} {:answer/id #uuid "8e0792d1-d0d7-58d4-acd0-2928fe61d064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of odd multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270498} {:answer/id #uuid "a19bd95b-bbd6-55a2-9a46-010b458abb4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is not a root of $p(x)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270704} {:answer/id #uuid "c3b75751-2d73-5678-8386-167ce0d8f782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a simple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270997} {:answer/id #uuid "f84b8666-1809-58bd-bfe6-8ab9488eb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84b8666-1809-58bd-bfe6-8ab9488eb61f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271447} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb90200a-e2bb-5eaf-ba24-af1df789d82c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "86545112-71e2-5627-8c9c-5a320524ae07" :question/math-academy-id "q-141144" :question/problem "![](images/40/4092c0852a573a0add69c6fb7b55f393d0767a5b6a2282a10194ea27cb1be9c4.png)\n\nA part of the graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements is true?" :question/worked-solution "| Multiplicity | Sketch | Sketch |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ \n\n (Simple Root) | ![](images/5a/5a0221f93c65a1eeb4503604a558a6da81c4c7af81c2c52cc35ecf3d189fe99f.png) | ![](images/d5/d556b4edf39181eebb7ec87c344d6f657a6875ecd2745405eaf5351ccaff6f96.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Even | ![](images/9d/9d14c2588da2ee9c9f5bbc32a70791146bed609ea0d6d5912aeaa2edab02e2d7.png) | ![](images/28/28ad9248a9a509e3a44937d0438fb2366f550a22360d01b9b6a29bcac41357e8.png) |\n| $>1$ \n\n (Multiple Root) \n\n Odd | ![](images/3a/3ae81378cb3c918dd5b7730519eb5a91e949f88eef9f03f4910f4ab8fb3cd5a9.png) | ![](images/95/9506a7ce4031f1f6244c0110c3235f66ea5652bb53548a032732869cf21f5997.png) |\n\nIt's possible to determine information regarding the multiplicities of the roots of a polynomial from its graph.\n\n- If the graph intersects the $x$-axis but is *not* tangent to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *simple* root $x=a.$\n\n- If the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ we have a *multiple* root $x=a.$ Furthermore:\n\n- If the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *even*.\n\n- If the graph crosses the $x$-axis at $x=a,$ the multiplicity of a root $x=a$ is *odd*.\n\nIn our case, the graph is *tangent* to the $x$-axis at $x=6.$ So, $x=6$ is a *multiple* root. \n\nFurthermore, the graph touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at $x=6.$ So, $x=6$ has *even* multiplicity.\n\nTherefore, $x=6$ is a multiple root of even multiplicity."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e4a34-a512-5ff4-abbc-66e6830d63d0" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Perfect Square is Isolated"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "110ee8cd-e830-5bb3-bab0-f16cb26fe2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269421} {:answer/id #uuid "13e31713-81cb-5dec-8652-93d586dd34fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269445} {:answer/id #uuid "29d7d2be-f02c-521d-b752-bf360c856fdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+3{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269648} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb20cc7-de16-5bcf-8e1b-43826770ca5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270109} {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84d763b-739b-535d-8995-04b2349046b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+6{)}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271448} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee48d03a-569e-51f8-be78-887782d15f39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "48f5b61f-2f3f-5cda-a551-c1e861cdd220" :question/math-academy-id "q-141513" :question/problem "Rewrite the expression $({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3$ as the difference between a perfect square and a constant." :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the following formula:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2ay+{a}^{2}=(y+a{)}^{2}\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a perfect square in the parentheses that we can factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ {y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2} & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({y}^{2}+12y+{6}^{2})-3 & = \\\\ (y+6{)}^{2}-3 & .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "129c0ce9-31d0-501f-9253-202530581425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269432} {:answer/id #uuid "1e413b35-35e1-5ce6-a283-f4ff4b83d4f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+10{)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269522} {:answer/id #uuid "395d3e65-bf9a-53cf-a730-73e30942c7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269789} {:answer/id #uuid "899c0f97-acdd-567e-ab3c-221326e9fa7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}+12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270456} {:answer/id #uuid "d9c19f26-9f49-5230-b707-0daae3af6b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "395d3e65-bf9a-53cf-a730-73e30942c7aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5{)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269789} :answer-field/id #uuid "12b561ff-d7d5-56bf-bd70-881cdbd1aea3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7d3420b-3f28-5142-8868-756f1394ea88" :question/math-academy-id "q-141537" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${x}^{2}+10x+16.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+16\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $16$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+16\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+5{)}^{2}-9.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4bc4897-6558-52b5-bb24-657fa38a810c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400932} {:answer/id #uuid "62f6fb47-ecea-56a8-9070-83fc55e7254f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400933} {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} {:answer/id #uuid "bdeaea36-9352-52ca-8483-6b276a77d472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400935} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc66d56-1a8c-5626-b30a-e9774929570d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d43940b-e27a-5dff-9dca-bf6857baf92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=3,\\,q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400934} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bbc964-13ea-55a0-9065-a7c49f46fb65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400931}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd1c73ef-6818-5b51-baee-cca065c2f0da" :question/math-academy-id "q-141539" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+6x+14$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+14\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}-{3}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+3{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=3$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272304 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b8b2afd-fb57-5e61-8b75-209b9cfb00a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269948} {:answer/id #uuid "56189019-79f9-5478-aff6-fce083001fda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2{)}^{2}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270032} {:answer/id #uuid "5b5b5397-8f7e-5320-893a-7dfa34d502d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2{)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270081} {:answer/id #uuid "b8a21ffd-56c6-5136-bf66-bc5dc8d6441d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270901} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c6894a-0f18-540f-812c-d0d96b4ec938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4{)}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8a21ffd-56c6-5136-bf66-bc5dc8d6441d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2{)}^{2}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270901} :answer-field/id #uuid "072e8659-a896-55fe-8d54-28d07c46a912" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c43e9bd-a43d-5cc8-ba55-c5e66008e58c" :question/math-academy-id "q-141612" :question/problem "Complete the square on the quadratic expression ${y}^{2}-4y+10.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+10\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 2y+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-2{)}^{2}+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400937 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "08c6d8d6-3d86-5074-bf99-0cb744c9412a" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression with a Negative Linear Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d53fe67-7a37-5135-9bda-59dc26da7eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400939} {:answer/id #uuid "9b5c7488-0854-5209-9e72-78867a6a1be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400940} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec775e-a603-50a4-a821-b02cc8bdf4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-10,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400941} {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400942} {:answer/id #uuid "c0e81c67-49e3-5deb-9f3d-4bfdd5f4f433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=5,\\text{ }q=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f01cc1-7dda-5a0a-a09b-a1edcdb5224f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=-5,\\text{ }q=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400942} :answer-field/id #uuid "c559c184-d951-52c2-ae9b-1fd6b5cd4853" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a81398bc-73ed-504a-b8b3-ef512650d7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-141622" :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}-10y+30$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+30\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $30$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2}+30\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y-5{)}^{2}+5.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=-5$ and $q=5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e24f9c-d383-5b77-a22a-8e142a072f0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33162c16-a26a-585b-b93a-caff202d8543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269736} {:answer/id #uuid "472c723c-83ab-5626-ad0b-8949bf5249de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269915} {:answer/id #uuid "929563b1-603a-55ce-b34f-10a3c1c67754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270550} {:answer/id #uuid "dbafdf59-1e66-5dce-9f48-65bc473487a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271191} {:answer/id #uuid "f1de299d-a3c5-5ebc-80da-bb0051f2d8ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "929563b1-603a-55ce-b34f-10a3c1c67754" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "104" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270550} :answer-field/id #uuid "16c6ad9b-646e-5a06-a5b2-bcae2f52ebb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0de10d9-8ebd-58e0-b040-9c2f5e59c9c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-141812" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-8x=20.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-8x+16$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-8x+16=(x-4{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-8x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x & =20 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 4\\cdot x+{4}^{2}-{4}^{2} & =20 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2}-16 & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $16$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2}-16+16 & =20+16 \\\\ (x-4{)}^{2} & =36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-4{)}^{2} & =36 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-4{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{36} \\\\ |x-4| & =6 \\\\ x-4 & =\\pm 6 \\\\ x & =4\\pm 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =4-6=-2 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =4+6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}=(-2{)}^{2}+{10}^{2}=104.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272321 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068827 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3e24f9c-d383-5b77-a22a-8e142a072f0a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation Given a Corresponding Perfect Square"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08658d01-89d0-5f4d-8f01-ca95f49a8f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269345} {:answer/id #uuid "91635a34-3072-5810-bbe9-632fc13648c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270534} {:answer/id #uuid "b63daf1b-11dc-55f7-bcdf-962180261257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270887} {:answer/id #uuid "c16cb9a6-c3c8-5125-ae28-f529c2244338" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270983} {:answer/id #uuid "f5121dc9-a221-5173-bccc-27c79eacbf3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "50" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271416}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91635a34-3072-5810-bbe9-632fc13648c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270534} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f3ef694-6ed8-54b6-8d68-ea9692dda819" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36afe386-dcce-5814-bb16-64bd8f39ae6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-142015" :question/problem "Let ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ be the solutions of the equation ${x}^{2}-10x=-21.$ Find the value ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}.$ \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${x}^{2}-10x+25$ is a perfect square.*" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-10x+25=(x-5{)}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-10x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x & =-21 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5\\cdot x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =-21 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2}-25 & =-21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we isolate the perfect square by adding $25$ to both sides: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2}-25+25 & =-21+25 \\\\ (x-5{)}^{2} & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-5{)}^{2} & =4 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\ |x-5| & =2 \\\\ x-5 & =\\pm 2 \\\\ x & =5\\pm 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =5-2=3 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =5+2=7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}={3}^{2}+{7}^{2}=58.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096807 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e274fe1-7242-5a6c-8351-eeff9877d2bf" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Derivative of a Quotient of Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a9a5d2e-53a9-565b-a1fc-8cd26f86a3d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273163} {:answer/id #uuid "b701a9a5-29bb-5708-a957-88c300832439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273973} {:answer/id #uuid "e6a1fc64-941c-5d5b-ab84-b99a95f4f1dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274425} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dc9ac0-b688-5b5c-9541-86b4f9ef8848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274457} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d6ceb2-4c78-5a7d-9d25-5474bd89a51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274539}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3d6ceb2-4c78-5a7d-9d25-5474bd89a51c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274539} :answer-field/id #uuid "ececad6e-65a0-55c7-b5bb-cd62a469aefa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d8bc070b-a1af-5473-852c-4d138913925e" :question/math-academy-id "q-15664" :question/problem "![](images/e4/e4b9bd92b63d85b0336974285b6aa7a8a7beb8348cf368f65d805f7b4b70ffa2.png)\n\nGiven the plots of $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ above, find ${h}^{′}(1)$ where $h(x)=\\frac{f(x)}{g(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "The quotient rule for derivatives gives\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot g(x)-f(x)\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(1)=\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}}.\n$$\n\nFrom the graph above, we obtain that $f(1)=3$ and $g(1)=2.$\n\nAdditionally:\n\n- The slope of $f(x)$ on $x\\in (0,2)$ equals $1.$ So, ${f}^{′}(1)=1.$\n\n- The slope of $g(x)$ on $x\\in (0,3)$ equals $-1.$ So, ${g}^{′}(1)=-1.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(1) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(1)\\cdot g(1)-f(1)\\cdot {g}^{′}(1)}{(g(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot 2-3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275432 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0251566-4dfc-5786-b00a-ce609e5b2698" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1308c252-3f5f-5a02-be87-52593ac89917" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272558} {:answer/id #uuid "33cb8823-ce66-5677-84d7-89e4c81c03e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272846} {:answer/id #uuid "9ccbdf46-9ee5-5738-a68a-6a4c7e94d746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273746} {:answer/id #uuid "c35f06e3-925e-57a7-8e44-c7ad3fa649fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274082} {:answer/id #uuid "e42382ea-4217-5fb3-b74a-833c31fad439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274404}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e42382ea-4217-5fb3-b74a-833c31fad439" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274404} :answer-field/id #uuid "d811a9a8-c409-5b01-851f-64933cb4d3b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275015}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0221b117-6ad9-5a2f-80bb-79e143dc5ca6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158055" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=\\frac{5}{2},$ then $cot⁡(-x)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nOur graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176057 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275f4184-6614-5fb5-b945-c0a925615360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272735} {:answer/id #uuid "8a7df9b3-60b6-5ef6-8973-d6465d2aa3e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273582} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1e5805-1c6c-5d57-b929-84303eb5f147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273626} {:answer/id #uuid "ae068c2c-1302-562e-8e3f-5924d2a8dd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273888} {:answer/id #uuid "b395db12-bd50-557e-ae1c-0cff707eab03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b395db12-bd50-557e-ae1c-0cff707eab03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273938} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea032dc8-afc8-5574-9882-b42bbb63200d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a595d8eb-425e-5670-9569-3c6cc685c869" :question/math-academy-id "q-158059" :question/problem "If $cos⁡x=0,$ then $\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/23/2373daf4b5fd26f098bf90e998e2685db6a8b793e94ac9d80b08ea423cb535c0.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cos⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric over the $y$-axis. This means that $cos⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncos⁡(-x)=cos⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x+18\\pi ) & =cos⁡(-x+2\\pi \\cdot 9) \\\\  & =cos⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $cos⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡(-x) & =cos⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\pi cos⁡(-x+18\\pi )-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\pi \\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275436 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0251566-4dfc-5786-b00a-ce609e5b2698" :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Oddness Property of the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b4110e0-8cbe-509f-8768-e57ad22b3e55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272490} {:answer/id #uuid "0dfc4511-a4c7-5555-89b3-9b7095440042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272509} {:answer/id #uuid "8fd51020-1a5c-56db-8c08-63b63d83c079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273636} {:answer/id #uuid "bf721ced-4517-5626-a108-41e175743956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274043} {:answer/id #uuid "e6d362da-9dbc-5d76-bebd-946b5b66ed7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dfc4511-a4c7-5555-89b3-9b7095440042" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272509} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b3c6ee6-6e94-558d-95c0-4e54abe1c6d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274710}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2a79aaf7-1dcc-57ab-a0b7-6d93a2b468da" :question/math-academy-id "q-158064" :question/problem "If $cot⁡x=-6,$ then $\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi )+7=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/6e/6eb672fe024f024f20c7c3c80947d1b47bade8f998e705d6f411ae3f298ecde2.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $cot⁡x$ is $\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph has rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. This means that $cot⁡x$ is an *odd* function, and we have\n\n$$\ncot⁡(-x)=-cot⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi ) & =cot⁡(-x+\\pi \\cdot 15) \\\\  & =cot⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the oddness of $cot⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cot⁡(-x) & =-cot⁡x \\\\  & =-(-6) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}cot⁡(-x+15\\pi )+7 & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6+7 \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{2}+7 \\\\  & =3+7 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278271 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b346e7e1-b641-5a97-9677-84a0ff6ab2dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Values in a Data Set Given the Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ed36547-96e7-5784-9a0e-cef5f230e794" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275755} {:answer/id #uuid "60b2eba2-e4ea-55fb-b0a5-7949921702ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276344} {:answer/id #uuid "90863935-708d-5d8a-9767-701612197aa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276786} {:answer/id #uuid "a7774b83-c8c2-533c-9b91-ea243dc329ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276988} {:answer/id #uuid "b652cf44-f298-51b1-9814-ea015f204d32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7774b83-c8c2-533c-9b91-ea243dc329ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276988} :answer-field/id #uuid "bba5517c-87f3-5296-8e79-3699c931499e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88fbb37a-350b-51de-9b64-f5ff2acb9c93" :question/math-academy-id "q-160026" :question/problem "Given that the mean of the following data set is $10,$ find the value of $b:$ \n\n$$\nb,\\,1,\\,b,\\,1,\\,b\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=5$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =b+1+b+1+b \\\\  & =2+3b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (2+3b) \\\\  & =\\frac{2+3b}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=10,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =\\frac{2+3b}{5} \\\\ 50 & =2+3b \\\\ 48 & =3b \\\\ b & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115305 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b346e7e1-b641-5a97-9677-84a0ff6ab2dc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Missing Values in a Data Set Given the Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5e32c7-6b89-5822-855f-1dfb6a5a9745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275870} {:answer/id #uuid "2d86a5a3-7293-5268-b8e4-2c73a0117d10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275888} {:answer/id #uuid "4248bdf2-7c80-5f67-8d39-490fc7fd9035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276057} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4c24fa-3a89-5653-8566-bcbe05f0a9ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276432} {:answer/id #uuid "ff2ae68b-d06f-52a1-8b62-ccf349b47446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff2ae68b-d06f-52a1-8b62-ccf349b47446" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277719} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe8dea39-9fe7-5d93-8b7f-3596ae2988dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2ae95412-5543-5cd6-a29d-d4219b71ef9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-160028" :question/problem "Given that the mean of the following data set is $11,$ find the value of $c:$ \n\n$$\n(c-3),\\,6,\\,3,\\,c,\\,10,\\,20\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =(c-3)+6+3+c+10+20 \\\\  & =36+2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is given by the expression\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot (36+2c) \\\\  & =\\frac{36+2c}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we're told that $\\overset{―}{x}=11,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11 & =\\frac{36+2c}{6} \\\\ 66 & =36+2c \\\\ 30 & =2c \\\\ c & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278276 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adfc527d-4347-5741-a81c-fa91c50d5d74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Data Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5be0bc62-d602-5b32-8a2e-36d125dbe0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276301} {:answer/id #uuid "c0a1d8a5-8c5d-5e57-a888-62ddebfefa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277188} {:answer/id #uuid "e71d3129-f92a-5c5b-837b-240366c795c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277496} {:answer/id #uuid "faed2406-8996-50c6-b0ef-9ef87571e4b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277675} {:answer/id #uuid "fcb5e3e3-e49d-5444-9e56-c411ae1a8e1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277691}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0a1d8a5-8c5d-5e57-a888-62ddebfefa97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277188} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad91c49c-4a4d-571c-acb9-c533b9863422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4975910-186b-51eb-8386-78fc12db0a69" :question/math-academy-id "q-160052" :question/problem "A college basketball player scored the following number of points in the last five games he played:\n\n$$\n9,\\,11,\\,12,\\,8,\\,10\n$$\n\nWhat is the mean of these scores?" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=5$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}{x}_{i} & =9+11+12+8+10 \\\\  & =50.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 50 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278277 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115304 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "adfc527d-4347-5741-a81c-fa91c50d5d74" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Mean of a Data Set"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e67b2a5-0f28-5b12-888c-9ffba56fdcc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275597} {:answer/id #uuid "53c635b2-f846-551d-b578-e72691826950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276220} {:answer/id #uuid "67146a85-6286-573e-a37d-2c1c8f8de67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276398} {:answer/id #uuid "c72cf84a-4573-550f-a9d6-4a4ad1b52ddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277247} {:answer/id #uuid "e5554f5b-a5a2-59e4-88ec-a1c9997632f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53c635b2-f846-551d-b578-e72691826950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276220} :answer-field/id #uuid "23112a7c-974e-57c0-ab45-efd0be8b2843" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac9d78d0-acf8-5125-b9f8-810932b0249e" :question/math-academy-id "q-160054" :question/problem "A car salesman has sold the following quantities of a particular model of car in the last few weeks:\n\n$$\n3,\\,5,\\,6,\\,8,\\,5,\\,3\n$$\n\nWhat is the mean of these figures?" :question/worked-solution "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the mean is denoted by $\\overset{―}{x}$ and is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\n$$\n\nHere, there are $n=6$ data points. Adding up all the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} & =3+5+6+8+5+3 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "623f4440-ba1a-58ce-9ff2-ccdf591a0d30" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Decay Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e91d68c4-e924-5c0b-8892-337b390a9278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ca/cadd20667d93ddc9058703331d2442573b4febc0eb79b0c50749490a859d7006.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389583} {:answer/id #uuid "d252b9b0-032b-5d42-9ac1-9f37846b1397" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/4442c3b03668bf3d4b8090c56c4ad083e85aa4399a0326255fe2322f2dcc729b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389584} {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} {:answer/id #uuid "b9429aad-0aa1-50e2-95f4-b8ee60295084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b9/b934a5fcadb46619e6c3631e6f9d78c8a0f72beeb023f9696c01422503188586.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389586} {:answer/id #uuid "9f9ba8eb-d544-5202-91c3-233917e73327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eed323df68b293af1fa8ec575d4dca23f16fe01865b01c3fa9817eead7b66fd2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389587}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d19a1858-cafb-517d-ba52-283fda091cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/779c8384569a3cf6cec79186e69a657b4e23f98f2dd464a3879700b4c08fd27a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389585} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba892752-d68e-5265-b5cc-644accea6428" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389582}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1f2385ee-c24f-5a4c-9712-f5717ae06cfb" :question/math-academy-id "q-167668" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/e9/e992d80b1245e6fad1c1e2968c396c51ef863d56d36c184592f8025fc450fa99.png)\n\n- Reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/aa/aa0778c338e9b57aca82057edc1b984e051d05a3f5413c2b1c70c3874fcf063e.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $3$ units down to give $y=-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,-4)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/25/25b4ef6f489f19d81b1d54a3240faf5e99f20a9463aa2ce9bd2e39796bf39cb3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a316d2cb-bab1-518a-9a6c-3956bfcbcd26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401595} {:answer/id #uuid "9ec09427-50e7-5ea6-a6c5-d462ec4a977e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401596} {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401597} {:answer/id #uuid "ed368aed-25dd-5df6-b266-89719aec6636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401598} {:answer/id #uuid "d50e40c4-e8c2-5ba6-ad0e-dc0b92fad230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401599}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "685faf06-be0e-5f6e-869b-353ff052fead" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401597} :answer-field/id #uuid "00e70783-1025-5acd-82ba-24685ceb80f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401594}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b98dba6-8029-58a2-a81c-ae05e5ca1860" :question/math-academy-id "q-170347" :question/problem "![](images/71/71d9df0708a77639f3f7b2640ea2a03cebbfd72e6cfb3369c07d83a89eba6508.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$ \n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{3}{5})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-1.$ Therefore, $c=-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10110147-d53a-52b4-9412-60917bae18a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281834} {:answer/id #uuid "35ebf50d-9e51-5dd6-90ce-6b42609e1277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282167} {:answer/id #uuid "4a227723-7044-54e3-b22e-e4d9e959d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282319} {:answer/id #uuid "59445ca6-d8f8-5abc-befc-bb2cef4921d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282448} {:answer/id #uuid "f114926f-bae0-59d7-a3c0-0d8adbad48cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a227723-7044-54e3-b22e-e4d9e959d300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282319} :answer-field/id #uuid "59d527ed-62e9-59c3-b626-0a9ba794e766" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284082}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40f36d04-c36f-53cb-8067-f12e5e811687" :question/math-academy-id "q-170350" :question/problem "![](images/06/06ea604e70390627f9e98dbea5048f27bd1f63d0b10002bbd5518c000b5125bf.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=\\frac{4}{3},$ hence $c=\\frac{4}{3}.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3} & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{0}+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k+\\frac{4}{3} & =2 \\\\ k & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nk-c=\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{4}{3}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=\\frac{2}{3}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}+\\frac{4}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087759 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "54588722-bb80-54ca-b100-03aca39e0c17" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Argument of a Complex Number in the Fourth Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fc383aa-c6d8-5cb4-9f37-78f78398d296" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.84" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282112} {:answer/id #uuid "8bcd893c-01c1-52b0-8774-2ca408dfc3cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.99" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282899} {:answer/id #uuid "97b0eb56-a4d3-596c-a9c5-af16c716e2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283002} {:answer/id #uuid "e5691c8f-e6ed-5728-ace3-4968571878b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283691} {:answer/id #uuid "ef691221-3ed8-5189-8810-d04927763ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef691221-3ed8-5189-8810-d04927763ada" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283774} :answer-field/id #uuid "a32f94de-a65d-57b8-a1d7-93ea2655c742" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284216}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "876ee8ae-f199-5a6e-ad24-4a4c31d3be35" :question/math-academy-id "q-170494" :question/problem "Find, in radians to two decimal places, the argument of $z=9-6\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/1b/1bc5f38d44d9df32a7493f8d210bbc17d716d8a63316e3d730a1c4258d56fe46.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<arg⁡(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =arctan⁡(\\frac{6}{9})=arctan⁡(\\frac{2}{3})≈0.588\\,00.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =arg⁡(z)=2\\pi -0.588\\,00≈5.70\n$$\n\nto two decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284688 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096611 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8b7d0153-6e0a-5e19-83f1-960811dc5205" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "397833d7-9cc6-50ae-8de7-3a5674155a04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282196} {:answer/id #uuid "8d66fe18-2e0a-548c-a20c-052aa8a29cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282914} {:answer/id #uuid "cfc41fd0-6fd9-5709-9af2-b2dd6b711472" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283503} {:answer/id #uuid "df2ebee4-e1b4-5f85-bbbc-9c810b46a256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283637 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409257}]} {:answer/id #uuid "fe32be51-d0ac-5644-92b4-204731798028" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df2ebee4-e1b4-5f85-bbbc-9c810b46a256" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283637} :answer-field/id #uuid "34cf92cc-bcc5-56af-93bf-714a48258bfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5816a349-a320-5d09-93fc-2ee07a9147af" :question/math-academy-id "q-17247" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, using the addition and the power rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})-3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-3(2x) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(2{e}^{x}-6x)\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =2{e}^{0}-6\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1-0 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0beb1ecb-7649-51d5-8923-8085f1d146bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401629} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ec049-b8d0-5c17-a28c-05813122674f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401630} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d87b1b-17a5-523c-a973-f5e0ad287cd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401631} {:answer/id #uuid "ea4f4094-0cfd-5ae4-8308-88f0b83b2c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401632} {:answer/id #uuid "946c0adc-baba-5359-a975-69b7a89f4838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "aa5ec049-b8d0-5c17-a28c-05813122674f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401630} :answer-field/id #uuid "5088efea-7f9e-57d5-8f9c-14c688a0e06b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401628}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "104d7814-9b21-55a2-864d-c61ec56f5c8f" :question/math-academy-id "q-17392" :question/problem "Given that $y=2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x$, find the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the instantaneous rate of change at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nTaking the derivative, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{e}-ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2e{x}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{x})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2e\\cdot {1}^{e-1}-\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =2e-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cec59a22-5d6d-5de8-9f8a-45ef6bb808a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\,957\\,920" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389820} {:answer/id #uuid "afeaf0f3-14db-58e7-8c72-651f40583589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,581\\,580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389821} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb700f8-9524-59c1-89ab-917235da5475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,209\\,112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389822} {:answer/id #uuid "6cc04816-e879-5d68-b950-f374a65870ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,662\\,100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389823} {:answer/id #uuid "c82b7429-84d6-539e-b5c5-1835b7badc17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "750\\,020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389824}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "afeaf0f3-14db-58e7-8c72-651f40583589" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,581\\,580" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389821} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c7c9200-9af1-5711-bd23-c6a6abc5a745" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389819}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bfaea9e-ab13-55ff-a72c-f7e351269ac2" :question/math-academy-id "q-1781" :question/problem "How many different ways can a group of four be made from a room of eighty people?" :question/worked-solution "In general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_{n}{C}_{r}=\\frac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}.\n$$\n\nThere are $80$ people, so $n=80.$ We want to select $4$ of those people for each group, so we have $r=4.$\n\nTherefore, the number of different potential groups of four is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{80}{C}_{4} & =\\frac{80!}{(80-4)!\\,4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80!}{76!4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77\\cdot \\cancel{76!}}{\\cancel{76!}4!} \\\\  & =\\frac{80\\cdot 79\\cdot 78\\cdot 77}{4\\cdot 3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{37\\,957\\,920}{24} \\\\  & =1\\,581\\,580.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398638 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca7f6e86-a083-5dcd-beaa-8c3eae151bf1" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Multiplicity of a Root of a Cubic Polynomial Using Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49f83912-5796-573a-b936-a61ffb603ff7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5,\\,5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398640} {:answer/id #uuid "b6a7c8bb-09ff-5a0f-b690-cf0f292ac7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398641} {:answer/id #uuid "d012c549-4023-5194-9620-c38392bb0ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398642} {:answer/id #uuid "1ca07054-2359-5a47-9068-5f524310351e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398643} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f9a8cc-12c2-5db4-9c8e-f8300024f0d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6a7c8bb-09ff-5a0f-b690-cf0f292ac7ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398641} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a80fbe4-dbfb-530c-af35-e0f30a4a5542" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398639}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d601c130-3a93-5630-8b35-93cbbfae4d1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-178256" :question/problem "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125$ have multiplicity $2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-5)-25(x-5) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-25)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-5)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x-5{)}^{2}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284771 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059676 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "711f198b-26b4-5185-812a-102329d49313" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With the Tangent Trigonometric Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b4dddb2-8ce0-518b-941e-482ca5265f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282747} {:answer/id #uuid "bc54686a-049f-5040-b7a9-b8062cc1c750" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283321} {:answer/id #uuid "be37be01-215e-5391-bca4-f872aefba533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283341} {:answer/id #uuid "ea403761-0f48-5b27-a322-4bdbfaf43080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283735} {:answer/id #uuid "f3762c53-5b99-5615-8c9e-e78f50dd46f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283817}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b4dddb2-8ce0-518b-941e-482ca5265f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282747} :answer-field/id #uuid "33d75bdd-6edb-5b58-9b7f-46c8d0ecd14e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284025}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a05f390f-2f4a-5c91-9fbd-0dbf4e6d9e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-17912" :question/problem "Calculate the instantaneous rate of change of the function $f(x)=-tan⁡x$ when $x=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change  when $x=\\pi $ is given by the value of the derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ at $x=\\pi .$ \n\nFirst, we compute the derivative, using the fact that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(tan⁡x)={sec}^{2}⁡x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-tan⁡x) \\\\  & =-{sec}^{2}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=\\pi ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\pi ) & =-{sec}^{2}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =-1\\cdot (-1)^{2} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the instantaneous rate of change is $-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284784 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74677256-e307-5d49-b7e0-c3f4e79cf818" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Ways That N Objects Can Be Arranged"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08737316-aa20-5df6-851c-dce808b637e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281762} {:answer/id #uuid "18fd45dc-1ede-51cf-9585-369b367a8961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281914} {:answer/id #uuid "82a108e9-b949-5ec3-82d9-001193cc0c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282820} {:answer/id #uuid "b939e1e9-b6b5-5e42-8a94-194f830d95e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283290} {:answer/id #uuid "ea97fa87-edd6-5cc7-984b-14ae7f4f6d7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b939e1e9-b6b5-5e42-8a94-194f830d95e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283290} :answer-field/id #uuid "6814504a-adfe-5300-8bd4-6599a98f28d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284106}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53fbad36-9779-558b-839c-131c419555eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-1804" :question/problem "Suppose that $5$ pictures are to be hung from left to right in a hallway. In how many possible ways can they be hung?" :question/worked-solution "There are $n=5$ pictures, so the number of possible arrangements is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5! & =5\\times 4\\times 3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there are $120$ ways of arranging the $5$ pictures."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287884 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059675 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Derivative of an Expression With Sine or Cosine Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "203dab4a-3058-5e0e-90fa-52dcc35d2407" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285078} {:answer/id #uuid "46da0d85-3682-54d0-b58f-5f7b61e746ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2cos⁡x+4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285442} {:answer/id #uuid "55c1f225-5772-53df-9957-91ede62f7763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285577} {:answer/id #uuid "66e054b3-95bb-5323-86e6-76b98c6154cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285725} {:answer/id #uuid "92c0eef2-67dc-58fb-baa0-cc77ea56c821" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4cos⁡x-2sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55c1f225-5772-53df-9957-91ede62f7763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285577} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1a13dae-3ed1-5b21-b23c-46bc3ddb17e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3974f0a3-ea97-56a9-a9e2-a7e59e1e84b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-1847" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x)=$" :question/worked-solution "We know that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(sin⁡x)=cos⁡x,$ and that $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(cos⁡x)=-sin⁡x.$ Applying these formulas, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4cos⁡x+2sin⁡x) & =4(-sin⁡x)+2(cos⁡x) \\\\  & =2cos⁡x-4sin⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390340 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46b4837f-6ba5-5812-8dc7-4671fcd6b20d" :knowledge-point/title "Plotting a Vertically Scaled Exponential Decay Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4933cab4-6c6f-5e1e-b9e9-375a48b8bbd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c7ef699169e521845d96c6b28392271c27cca116b449277c66817dc2dbb6491.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390342} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd7f842-ec45-57a8-93f4-8edce627ac5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/33/3357b2cf67b40afa54b081c67855f28a74d71209459382c0e421b6e2da7d114f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390343} {:answer/id #uuid "c26f47ff-1aa3-50e2-840e-6408b7c4640f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dcec9bbf7c5a1badb7e0193a16e802fe0b5558c1a076f65da60d