es [{:answer/id #uuid "5446db9d-f32b-503a-98c3-71bd4b13a940" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9{x}^{1/4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285562} {:answer/id #uuid "72b849ec-c73e-51c6-8bd6-7d57955bf8d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[3]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285843} {:answer/id #uuid "93d4bb13-717f-50aa-afa5-f6147b1c9f75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}-9{x}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286135} {:answer/id #uuid "a7da2020-89e3-5d5f-bbdb-6fb669e629c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{9{x}^{-1/4}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286320} {:answer/id #uuid "e9029ff2-9352-53d1-a4ea-86df802fa96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9029ff2-9352-53d1-a4ea-86df802fa96d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286919} :answer-field/id #uuid "8320dfd3-7a24-5485-a9b9-30a0c49ac165" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287423}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76292ca7-955e-5da2-801e-cf1602a2e16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-1856" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for $y=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}.$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we apply the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}-3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-2})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2){x}^{(-2-1)}-3\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{(3/4-1)} \\\\  & =-4{x}^{-3}-\\frac{9}{4}{x}^{-1/4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{{x}^{3}}-\\frac{9}{4\\sqrt[4]{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186060177 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6eef35a-3502-5429-bd71-fb5bd0e8efc8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Extrema on Transformed Curves"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} {:answer/id #uuid "874eb524-3ed8-5726-b354-81594d6ba038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286028} {:answer/id #uuid "8da77226-b79f-5aa1-9fbb-eff1ddcce302" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286083} {:answer/id #uuid "de9a3fa5-07d3-5f60-9264-81029b284219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286813} {:answer/id #uuid "e77beded-c213-529c-9af4-9172511b2a92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,8)." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286904}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "132ada2d-225c-55ef-ad34-507a30f3f6af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284958} :answer-field/id #uuid "e490dbf9-e5ff-5dfa-a559-074583566e32" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c0bb866-f5d6-5c26-9148-409419fa5279" :question/math-academy-id "q-186866" :question/problem "![](images/f4/f4da78b7a12ed475b64c1f824e8cecdf36657c6d174f0db770219fd8dd6c0801.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the global minimum and maximum of the function $y=f(x).$ What is the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)?$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the graph of $y=-2f(x+1)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by \n\n- translating it by $1$ unit to the left, then\n\n- stretching it by a scale factor of $2$ parallel to the $y$-axis, and finally\n\n- flipping it across the $x$-axis.\n\nSince one of these transformations flips the graph across the $x$-axis, the combined transformation maps the global **maximum** of $y=f(x)$ to the global **minimum** of $y=-2f(x+1).$ So, we need to find the image of $(-1,4),$ the global maximum of $y=f(x),$ under the transformation. \n\nTo plot $y=-2f(x+1),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x)$ that passes through $(-1,4).$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph horizontally by $1$ unit to the left and get the graph of $y=f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-1,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-1-1,4)=(-2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we stretch the result by a scale factor of $2$ along the $y$-axis and get the graph of $y=2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,4)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,2\\cdot 4)=(-2,8).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we flip the result vertically across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-2f(x+1).$ After this transformation, the point $(-2,8)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(-2,-1\\cdot 8)=(-2,-8).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the global minimum of $y=-2f(x+1)$ is $(-2,-8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287917 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7995b377-1de4-5126-aa29-8cc30b4918f8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Slope of the Tangent at a Point of a Function Involving Reciprocal Trigonometric Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d15acc-1672-5ec4-bb78-f3474884b897" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284830} {:answer/id #uuid "48fabaf5-be8a-589c-8cf0-074c455a9240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285458} {:answer/id #uuid "63f73e2e-4004-5fc8-9825-f8337f6c2b07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{6}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285697} {:answer/id #uuid "766bb2aa-4af5-5e09-aed3-a777505c1a5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}+10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285891} {:answer/id #uuid "e40e7c86-1ed2-5d5e-9940-61e65ceaec5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e40e7c86-1ed2-5d5e-9940-61e65ceaec5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286873} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8570317-ecab-5ed9-a868-188d2c2ead8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287627}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9daa906-697e-5ad9-9a96-96e98679f403" :question/math-academy-id "q-1904" :question/problem "Find the slope of $y=f(x)$ at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ if $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we calculate the derivative using the sum rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{d}{dx}(-{x}^{2}+5csc⁡x) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-x)+5(-csc⁡xcot⁡x) \\\\  & =-2x-5csc⁡xcot⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the derivative and get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) & =-2(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-5csc⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})cot⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-5\\cdot 2\\cdot \\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}-10\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401805 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115191 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9699341b-c4e2-547f-8d98-bcda30567272" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Expressions Involving the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a34f86b-faa2-5844-9680-3f6951dc4ecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401807} {:answer/id #uuid "63214331-a811-5bf2-8cb7-4da5c8c1d678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{cot}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}csc⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401808} {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401809} {:answer/id #uuid "5a35c415-50b4-5005-8173-6121abbd643e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{2}csc⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401810} {:answer/id #uuid "cb8e226f-a28c-5b1a-b7df-6f312bfd06a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x+8{x}^{3}tan⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2285f6af-f131-5a62-954e-164127014717" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401809} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7aeb284-4f80-510f-8935-07a7e23c626b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401806}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee4e083b-fcc9-5eb5-9a27-ac52f9a97afd" :question/math-academy-id "q-1913" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ if $y=-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We calculate the derivative using the product rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{x}^{4}cot⁡x) \\\\  & =(-8{x}^{3})(cot⁡x)+(-2{x}^{4})(-{csc}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =-8{x}^{3}cot⁡x+2{x}^{4}{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064179 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e617f241-254b-5f70-9937-5883b6f28c4f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Secant-Tangent and Cosecant-Cotangent"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16d4843e-1503-5b14-9ef4-6d9bd866b768" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284994} {:answer/id #uuid "5f513476-ecb1-5481-9bb0-8cd729eba6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡xcot⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285660} {:answer/id #uuid "c159be5b-9ba9-50ec-90b5-6beba2ba516f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "csc⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286558} {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb10ca-14c4-5f21-a345-f92c3250e22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a909e4-a442-5f93-bb1c-8af5b5eeb86f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-csc⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbdb10ca-14c4-5f21-a345-f92c3250e22c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sec⁡\\theta +C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286788} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5759735-03db-58c8-afc1-42a56314fd9a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8eefdd68-c49a-5b6b-9578-f46e438f3aac" :question/math-academy-id "q-2038" :question/problem "$\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta =sec⁡\\theta +C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (-sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta )\\text{d}\\theta  & =-\\int sec⁡\\theta tan⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-(sec⁡\\theta )+C \\\\  & =-sec⁡\\theta +C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290323 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110599 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29005c66-9f30-5862-926f-8a09320d404c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Slope-Intercept Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f2f9488-176e-5ee3-ad8e-2db95b1123f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288350} {:answer/id #uuid "3f03d5e0-e5b1-53b3-ab18-0d7e379a24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288461} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf2b0ef-9634-5c51-86e7-60eaa13bc504" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289396} {:answer/id #uuid "cea07821-893d-5fac-a3e9-4d99a591dc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289410} {:answer/id #uuid "f789d1aa-f223-5771-a67e-fc052240756a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f03d5e0-e5b1-53b3-ab18-0d7e379a24cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288461} :answer-field/id #uuid "aed86ac2-1482-5201-9d78-def088012c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da3fa1e4-9073-5723-b4d9-3e3d9f56d008" :question/math-academy-id "q-215890" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nThe line $y=x+\\frac{1}{2}$ is in slope-intercept form, and its slope is $1.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=-x+9$ has a slope of $-1.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n1\\cdot (-1)=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=-x+9$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064178 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating Sine and Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346358f5-312e-5c8a-9238-6be633b31e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sin⁡x-cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288379} {:answer/id #uuid "419bbacf-36c2-544f-888a-5bf28372bd28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288478} {:answer/id #uuid "70205983-7a23-51c1-a345-bf2ec479a0bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2sin⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288812} {:answer/id #uuid "ba4296e8-700e-5668-8c90-e562531a6962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289261} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a193d2-0ce7-5a28-86c4-834fae7f7271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x-cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba4296e8-700e-5668-8c90-e562531a6962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289261} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c59fbc8-d124-507b-af30-f117e1976ad1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d70818e9-1705-53df-abe3-9e6e72f287c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-2161" :question/problem "$\\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-cos⁡x+C, \\\\ \\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =sin⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (cos⁡x-sin⁡x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int cos⁡x\\,\\text{d}x-\\int sin⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =sin⁡x-(-cos⁡x)+C \\\\  & =sin⁡x+cos⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39f1030c-14e2-59e9-a189-ae72636d7cec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}+\\frac{{5}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288429} {:answer/id #uuid "831fe1aa-d1a9-59de-914e-66c8e45135cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x}ln⁡2+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288914} {:answer/id #uuid "a92a1f84-319a-5ffc-912d-da344601d75d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}}{5}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289153} {:answer/id #uuid "bfec5a66-eb21-510f-ac20-724d2da23f30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{x}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289301} {:answer/id #uuid "f611671a-7317-5307-aefb-d9046cadfb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f611671a-7317-5307-aefb-d9046cadfb32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289655} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b01bf45-11e5-550e-91d5-6d78dcabad9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3b4a92d-a353-507a-becb-bee354a44152" :question/math-academy-id "q-2277" :question/problem "$\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{e}^{x}+{2}^{x})\\,\\text{d}x & =5\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {2}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}+\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129080 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "021e3349-c54d-55a8-9095-902bba933660" :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Pythagorean Theorem to the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "041bb980-2220-5c0f-b904-f70291dc858b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288059} {:answer/id #uuid "1bcd216a-5d96-580d-89a1-1300b816ea9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{29}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288211} {:answer/id #uuid "6428772c-31cb-5d11-ad40-b384b65de264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288738} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ab4c14-8b8f-5d7f-941d-c5430187106a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289135} {:answer/id #uuid "b4335ae8-54ab-5f8c-98b6-6b1f10f60c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{33}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289224}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6428772c-31cb-5d11-ad40-b384b65de264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288738} :answer-field/id #uuid "94cc4b6d-4396-5dd4-a8e0-bcf717fb1b6e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "808fb241-7b41-5e43-96f3-08a62164f95c" :question/math-academy-id "q-23289" :question/problem "![](images/89/8904a5ebafade54a46cee77a2e20cac255c888d257ef1c208b2def32d247f2cd.png)\n\nThe point $P$ is located on the unit circle, as shown above. The $y$-coordinate of $P$ is $\\frac{11}{12}.$ What is the $x$-coordinate of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "Since the point $P$ lies on the unit circle, it must satisfy the Pythagorean theorem: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=\\frac{11}{12}$ into the above and solving for $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+{(\\frac{11}{12})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}+(\\frac{121}{144}) & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1-\\frac{121}{144} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{23}{144} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{23}{144}} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we select the negative answer since $x$ is negative in the second quadrant. So our final answer is $x=-\\frac{\\sqrt{23}}{12}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290468 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110600 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e732f270-1d6f-5ff8-8792-d9b329ea858d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Two Lines in Standard Form are Perpendicular"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b644ab2-4c21-5aeb-9032-26a79415192f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288444} {:answer/id #uuid "540e1587-3f3e-52a8-8424-5abf66ff30e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{3}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288610} {:answer/id #uuid "70b1df14-3de9-50cb-8b16-2a2eec5b3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816} {:answer/id #uuid "959ae715-31b5-5f44-931c-f40632872d6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289030} {:answer/id #uuid "c2d44e35-06ab-5ae0-8bfd-256c7f07808a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{3}x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289319}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70b1df14-3de9-50cb-8b16-2a2eec5b3954" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288816} :answer-field/id #uuid "990087b9-37dc-5d9d-b34d-123762544320" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d4eec6d2-1c29-5a4c-9b34-ea67f65dce06" :question/math-academy-id "q-23621" :question/problem "Which of the following lines is perpendicular to $x+3y=6?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nTo find the slope of the given line $x+3y=6,$ we convert to slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3y & =6 \\\\ 3y & =-x+6 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn slope-intercept form, the slope is the $x$-coefficient. So, the slope of the given line is $-\\frac{1}{3}.$\n\nLooking at the answer choices, the line $y=3x$ has a slope of $3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n(-\\frac{1}{3})\\cdot 3=-1.\n$$\n\nSince the slopes multiply to $-1,$ we conclude that the line $y=3x$ is perpendicular to the given line."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290470 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b9cfaf6-9a1a-5e8a-ba94-ac0ace22d609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x-2y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288209} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0ae802-61bf-59f5-8b33-d414ac86732a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288552} {:answer/id #uuid "5a9e515d-8988-5114-8e78-b9e0df04aced" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+3y=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288663} {:answer/id #uuid "6463122b-ca6b-5780-a23a-d3c73e619707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-3y=-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288741} {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a46eb07-3565-5f66-8f29-3f6a3b2e30be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x+2y=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289064} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec350e8b-707f-561e-b0a8-e7f213d56fbc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96f3c704-d5cd-55de-a550-62294d23b6d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-23631" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line containing the point $(-1,1)$ that is perpendicular to $2x-3y=-5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $2x-3y=-5$ in its slope-intercept form, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-3y & =-5 \\\\ -3y & =-2x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\frac{2}{3}x+\\frac{5}{3}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\frac{2}{3},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and pass through the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,1).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x-(-1)) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}(x+1) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2y & =-3x-1 \\\\ 3x+2y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "222e0bdd-c45c-5f9b-ae54-71cdb6646a9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x-y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290780} {:answer/id #uuid "93fc4b47-47c7-5ebf-9229-bb1de3d6fc45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+5y=26" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291272} {:answer/id #uuid "96366abc-617a-5664-be0a-124e181d78a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+y=10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291284} {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd3655-249b-55c7-ad7e-aa78ebaf2966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291519} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c2f176-99d3-5c0e-91c7-0a77ab6cde83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+4y=19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0dd3655-249b-55c7-ad7e-aa78ebaf2966" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+2y=11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291519} :answer-field/id #uuid "f929ad97-39dd-50e0-b2f2-34f577615f96" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9bf24f67-6184-5bd5-8b16-92247aa73ac4" :question/math-academy-id "q-252524" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes, which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(2,2)$ and $(5,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{8-2}{5-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (2)=-1\\,⇒\\,m=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =-\\frac{1}{2}(x-3) \\\\ 2(y-4) & =-(x-3) \\\\ 2y-8 & =-x+3 \\\\ 2y & =-x+11 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we want to write the equation in standard form. So, we will bring the $x$ term to the left-hand side and multiply both sides of the equation by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x+y & =\\frac{11}{2} \\\\ 2\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}x+y) & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{2} \\\\ x+2y & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292672 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059668 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0bd940e7-fccc-5811-b07a-11e4843b1785" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} {:answer/id #uuid "487a2755-1c9b-50dc-a9ef-825d319c08d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290930} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5b7f1c-6ca6-519d-9324-afd97504caa0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291021} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbeb462-5ad4-5751-9859-039c2c20e85a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291457} {:answer/id #uuid "f4debbbb-a978-5aa2-807b-d09d5e6771d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291662}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33c00c15-b13b-5bcc-8be2-21e93f4c417e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290839} :answer-field/id #uuid "337977c2-17f5-5416-91d9-a9bbf15f1e4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291907}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6eb852b1-3670-5b7e-89d9-1b29c405f01a" :question/math-academy-id "q-26594" :question/problem "The point  $(3,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x).$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)-1?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)-1,$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- First, we take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the graph across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(1))=(3,-1).\n$$\n\n- Finally, we shift the result vertically by $1$ unit down and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-1)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-1-1)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,1)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)$, we have that $f(3)=1.$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)-1.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)-1 \\\\  & =-(1)-1 \\\\  & =-1-1 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292673 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f5bb3ff-2bf2-59a6-a9dc-028e36b29e5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290821} {:answer/id #uuid "6a0dffee-d291-5706-b099-bf26056b0a79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291085} {:answer/id #uuid "745f11b3-4abb-5778-b95c-05bfed92c9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291130} {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} {:answer/id #uuid "f811fc7c-86fc-528d-a6a8-cba9ceee139f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291677}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "add1ba6b-56e1-507d-81c4-d7cc7dd66d71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291387} :answer-field/id #uuid "e46bb93d-f096-5e20-be4c-e4f183d28feb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ee92c98-a45f-55db-8092-7db7c00e5a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-26596" :question/problem "The point  $(3,2)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-2.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+2?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nWe first find a point corresponding to $(3,2)$ on $y=f(x).$ Then, we find a corresponding point on $y=-f(x)+2:$\n\n- To plot the graph of $y=f(x),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-2.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $2$ units up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,2)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(3,2+2)=(3,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, to plot the graph of $y=-f(x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x).$\n\n- Then, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(3,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(3,-(4))=(3,-4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we shift the result vertically by $2$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ After this transformation, the point $(3,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(3,-4+2)=(3,-2).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(3,2)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-2$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(3)-2=2\\,⟹\\,f(3)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+2.$ Substituting $x=3$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(3)+2 \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(3,-2)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390514 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1db17739-2def-5e37-af22-164593bd5829" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/61cd6304483770e91658f37cd8ac3dbf57c58f25c9a631ab45a56cd706d381ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390516} {:answer/id #uuid "9904a233-df69-591c-90af-01e22fb617bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/31/31f0d95293939e141e0ebff2aaad6223a9a82188e892441906ae09c530919b1d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390517} {:answer/id #uuid "acd865cc-8ca0-5df0-b0c5-c2cd29210f44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/dfb46737087c1196ce079607220107a88857d6ca139d0d5c53b181795fe1344a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390518} {:answer/id #uuid "ddc2a988-d900-5cd3-a9a0-9a6ecf303b6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ee/eef682605cf7486a9d8fa23cd508705e70443622164eeb2c253ca342bbce92cb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390519} {:answer/id #uuid "fffb642d-e2f2-5134-92b3-6bde0b2a9298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bce2bc405efee4fc0fc0f8cd53a3f230fe407f1232c98237ced4ce64743a8499.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390520}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fffb642d-e2f2-5134-92b3-6bde0b2a9298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bce2bc405efee4fc0fc0f8cd53a3f230fe407f1232c98237ced4ce64743a8499.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390520} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe0ebeea-e82f-58d4-a5b0-0f856e91026c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390515}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "63caaf01-44b4-5dc6-a569-9e3a3a5c8859" :question/math-academy-id "q-26811" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={7}^{x}+4?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={7}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/cd/cd8226319b26b3fef6db6e98efc187b1c474c91bc0252e424151791fda373b95.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={7}^{x}+4,$ we take the graph of $y={7}^{x}$ and shift it $4$ units up. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,5),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$\n\n![](images/85/85cc1403ab74e2df97669a7c3363d1abddf6d093c358e888a69b655f1ca7450a.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390521 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd63cb30-a236-546e-82ac-08bce09c88f7" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e4093346-6cc0-5f63-9423-7b76da2a672f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1d/1d80c585063f3cd30a6ec1f877e0f2add2bf109bc54506e4b6c9e44a7b5019d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390523} {:answer/id #uuid "fd28d0fd-5ee9-586e-ade7-f559ef7573ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bb/bbdfe3e538e840385c082f16ca2cdfd07a7731c561d05cab6e7a25f487cdf463.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390524} {:answer/id #uuid "be9702e6-d21c-5c09-a96e-1267b5361dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd11656e3dae8a4976b1051a135b4bead1e5afc4beb21fa7541571892a037500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390525} {:answer/id #uuid "35b1a419-9084-5fab-a034-b69305bdd882" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/66/6674add26b7a15f5b09c53a832d649979484f0ffd41b440069dd5230cd9a5187.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390526} {:answer/id #uuid "724df295-2359-5b8b-ab62-27d9d0e10f5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a3/a30938f3d3af2fd70a0ee9498d7d99973d5386f062f22aaa8a028e9e18b3fb57.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9702e6-d21c-5c09-a96e-1267b5361dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/cd/cd11656e3dae8a4976b1051a135b4bead1e5afc4beb21fa7541571892a037500.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390525} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb7b8b6-ddf5-57db-b6c9-dfd64245bb49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390522}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5100b042-b82c-5834-8756-fafba09daf0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-26815" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={6}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/29/29d270676a48f4cf40625ef3ec50b0323c9c99389b2a7d080881727eda28de56.png)\n\nTo sketch the graph of $y={6}^{x}-0.75,$ we take the graph of $y={6}^{x}$ and shift it $0.75$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,0.25),$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-0.75.$\n\n![](images/3b/3b141be99e5c01c70d6f6f04f4adf06741f1dc0fcef80c73b52e3dba1ae44a87.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292696 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} {:answer/id #uuid "931ab8d4-be2c-5e4d-92d6-fc6433eec3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291265} {:answer/id #uuid "9767e111-d1e9-5924-9dc2-6f58d60a1d5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291290} {:answer/id #uuid "bb01b60e-6bb3-5d81-89d4-9256edcd3ff6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291443} {:answer/id #uuid "d869766d-8d2f-5348-b322-4dd0c0dc459d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291536}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043c68c8-e391-5780-a3ff-6d80dc52b081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290656} :answer-field/id #uuid "c15b4588-e9b5-5b8e-af3b-02394e3239c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "69137884-05b3-53be-9c7c-95b53b1ed329" :question/math-academy-id "q-27009" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=cos⁡x?$ \n\n- The function intersects the $x$-axis at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n- The function has a minimum value at  $x=2\\pi .$\n- The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=0.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cos⁡x.$\n\n![](images/9c/9c77bec4ebb04668fbff5176418ed9c09143a8660879fdf523a158c5f109006c.png)\n\nLet's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ the function intersects the $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement II is false. The function has a maximum at $x=2\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function intersects the $y$-axis at $y=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390531 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5145dde8-60d2-54b7-ad8d-2892ff5d3963" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4161037f51b8c4d95037c3ff28d7e6afecb354d4e0fabc22e5dd819bb65b6cc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390533} {:answer/id #uuid "6680ac81-034b-5243-a46f-e14b15ca391f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a4/a4511ffac1899bdeb512b0b50b50b6a121477f486ef7e2efc83697fd9dddb8d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390534} {:answer/id #uuid "2d7b451c-b477-571c-b8f0-b6b471ddf99b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/953bb642696a6b9c934627f978d150d27adbc7f73779a3d017c2ba714ff0835e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390535} {:answer/id #uuid "6fd2d67a-323e-5832-a842-a36b0a6fce73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6034af21a31fdfdbaff6760ab5dd97bb227d0538a475aef9e5c14a1559e16b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390536} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbecea5-be5b-5a1e-bd55-ff4ec8267187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df2b0819dabd55db60af06fecb52e19a013b471ab632484d4066ef5e011ccfe5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390537}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3fbecea5-be5b-5a1e-bd55-ff4ec8267187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df2b0819dabd55db60af06fecb52e19a013b471ab632484d4066ef5e011ccfe5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390537} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe628ffa-6fae-5342-9742-f9cf2475f662" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390532}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4cf19722-da75-5d66-8864-81b9e6bc4f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-27477" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/f0/f02afa6dd2d1c107fd72b1221fddb7f6c698506deb834cad0dc11af1bebb18e6.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $\\frac{1}{10}$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-\\frac{1}{10},$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,\\frac{9}{10}).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{10})}^{x}-\\frac{1}{10}$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/57/57539870ff45bae449d3ad7c11580d9c8068cfe76ac98c2f849ee38494288036.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068900 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ade855-4b16-514b-b44a-1d6b9d61ef28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c3666f3-ba98-51a9-b678-f25e043d3a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401917} {:answer/id #uuid "e8039e0c-f293-5ce4-b395-a4064a0685bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}-2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401918} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f24666-89d0-5332-bb2a-21f966094be5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}-3.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401919} {:answer/id #uuid "a479a80c-f0e1-5b4f-9f67-06b99cd086bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401920} {:answer/id #uuid "266f8f59-63e6-5980-a4b9-749b4fb8201c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "266f8f59-63e6-5980-a4b9-749b4fb8201c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401921} :answer-field/id #uuid "828a7f8a-1289-571e-8aa4-3a396be14d44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6948e752-4360-51fe-b06f-c6885f4f282f" :question/math-academy-id "q-27482" :question/problem "![](images/c0/c091fc5d742ddca3090725974f00ff7da77b1530d601bb82e288518b27970b0a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c.$ Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve." :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}$ is $y=0.$\n\nThe vertical translation $y={(\\frac{1}{9})}^{x}+c$ shifts the asymptote to $y=c.$\n\nIn the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.5.$ Therefore, $c=2.5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f70e31a-a6d8-57be-8930-fe9e46798cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390547} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd43261-0c67-5dec-abd5-e030f8c694d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f6/f63e49a2e8f4a0702841eb132093bf6f69b8405af25719ea8f5abbf2bd691122.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390548} {:answer/id #uuid "c08648ea-dc6d-5578-89fa-a0be944fb62c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c6/c64e79b7c0069aaf7d4393a6879612f58f48103247b1ba7efef3531dc3986b93.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390549} {:answer/id #uuid "84311dad-8f82-5f9c-ab3a-b304e0b27aae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/57/571e2268e6b7f41e80b71c8764d321c298fe58232e5b4c5a84ce309fa0dd3530.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390550} {:answer/id #uuid "8b546f29-cf64-542e-8f37-af2b900bcda8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3d/3d7af2718ba7c30956218a00da51e77db950183bd2a85dbe74107e493fd5f03b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f70e31a-a6d8-57be-8930-fe9e46798cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390547} :answer-field/id #uuid "b29207c3-5e76-521f-8c52-fed97e9cf151" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab17c05b-3a04-5ac4-9de0-6246c2badd83" :question/math-academy-id "q-27540" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3?$" :question/worked-solution "We start with plotting the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e6/e648e3a5f0c844d6596d4848629c3e6749647228116bcc5da6fce250895141d5.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $\\frac{1}{2}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/378016bbd17371e3dfee1e80934e76b1357139d405cb320ac5befa252da82b28.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{2}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{x}-3.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $3$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,\\frac{1}{2})$ to $(0,-\\frac{5}{2})$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-3.$\n\n![](images/e5/e545a84145ac0a139ee5ecdf9692c20837d211f9a70fd79ad33dd13f60c856f7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186295268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110601 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f3d7d62-686b-59c4-9ea1-695c6be3180b" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Unknown Parameters Given Two Perpendicular Lines"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55f88508-da2b-5f50-bc9a-a9d26b49e411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293282} {:answer/id #uuid "a5105cfc-01bb-5d42-a770-d43d047ecbd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293581} {:answer/id #uuid "c8156b99-af7d-5f7b-b0a7-f383c4b1a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} {:answer/id #uuid "c91b613b-66f6-5d95-9a11-12d165d11100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293713} {:answer/id #uuid "f1ecb80b-49f9-59d8-9960-66dfad7c060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8156b99-af7d-5f7b-b0a7-f383c4b1a5c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293707} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2a93840-02de-536d-9650-a5ee63bece85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294727}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fe99c4a9-356e-5f86-a2a8-a7dd24fba9f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-28328" :question/problem "Given that the lines $y=5qx+9$ and $y=-3x-8$ are perpendicular, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "For the two lines to be perpendicular, their slopes must multiply to $-1.$\n\nBoth lines are slope-intercept form, and the slope is the $x$-coefficient. \n\n- The slope of the line $y=5qx+9$ is $5q.$\n\n- The slope of the line $y=-3x-8$ is $-3.$\n\nMultiplying the slopes together, we have\n\n$$\n5q\\cdot (-3)=-1\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297159 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059677 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b268833-a892-5a14-ab75-38558f16bc57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295511} {:answer/id #uuid "bf7be3a8-94b4-5a24-adb6-b753140ab093" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "45" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296278} {:answer/id #uuid "d19d31e7-9654-53a0-8391-14a1f9103d85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296372} {:answer/id #uuid "d427a2bc-12c0-5bdc-b3d1-7a4dbeb358d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296383} {:answer/id #uuid "edf73d42-1443-5359-8707-64a0a6a850c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d427a2bc-12c0-5bdc-b3d1-7a4dbeb358d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296383} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0375b64-7629-58c5-bdc5-3b40af6de210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "88cefa50-35a4-5398-bbcf-d41d22641c1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-28556" :question/problem "Find ${g}^{″}(4)$ if $g(x)=4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7.$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={(4{x}^{2}\\sqrt{x}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & ={(4{x}^{5/2}-2x+7)}^{′} \\\\  & =4(\\frac{5}{2}){x}^{3/2}-2+0 \\\\  & =10{x}^{3/2}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & ={(10{x}^{3/2}-2)}^{′} \\\\  & =10(\\frac{3}{2}){x}^{1/2}-0 \\\\  & =15{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =15\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the second derivative at $x=4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(4) & =15\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =15(2) \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083388 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c16a30bc-0c1a-54f2-95d6-47edb0c85736" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Product of the Roots of a Quadratic Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2567084b-b026-5eb4-b95e-0227a0635f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295471} {:answer/id #uuid "32c4576b-1351-5ac7-8694-de0426c7c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295554} {:answer/id #uuid "71a3851f-e88d-5f38-b01f-fcaaad95d68b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-391}{41}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295893} {:answer/id #uuid "922397a2-e0b6-58f1-bde5-54e22966b2c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2)\\cdot 391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296054} {:answer/id #uuid "abf37a1f-02b7-5b10-aaaf-f70acb95ee5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32c4576b-1351-5ac7-8694-de0426c7c366" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-391" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295554} :answer-field/id #uuid "29b0ff42-3a4a-57e5-b225-3f319bf73f19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "edeac1f1-6a23-50a2-b07f-f8b62b91f0ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-288825" :question/problem "Consider the quadratic equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+41x-391=0.\n$$\n\n**Without** solving the equation, determine the product of its solutions." :question/worked-solution "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nFor the given quadratic equation, we have\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=41,\\,c=-391.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the product of the solutions is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{c}{a} & =\\frac{(-391)}{1}=-391.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110667 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce7ef1dd-58cc-57f5-9ff9-fc997f4280ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d716c0-c74a-57f6-833b-a5816bf99cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295386} {:answer/id #uuid "67139bf8-96d1-5cee-b658-3ffcabbeb7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295848} {:answer/id #uuid "68c9ab76-11b5-597a-b277-a022ff7d7ce0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295856} {:answer/id #uuid "8406301a-c3a9-5c90-b099-4f4b2c78ba3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295973} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a38ae3-a755-5134-b132-4a013f680737" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14d716c0-c74a-57f6-833b-a5816bf99cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295386} :answer-field/id #uuid "3cc8c0d6-c061-513b-98bf-4315a1ac025c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c4102b2c-f2ad-56ac-9dd6-7dd59719b972" :question/math-academy-id "q-28973" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true in relation to the function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}?$\n\n- Its domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )$\n- Its range is $y\\in (0,\\infty )$\n- It is a decreasing function" :question/worked-solution "Let's start by recalling the graph of ${(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bbffed32708d30355ed3592deae845d1a84cd1f1772ef1d0fae7ac39518295.png)\n\nLet's go through the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ is defined for all input values $x.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The set of possible outputs of $f(x)={(\\frac{2}{7})}^{x}$ consists of all the positive numbers.\n\n- Statement III is true. For $x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ the graph of $y=f(x)$ falls. Therefore, $y=f(x)$ is a decreasing function.\n\nIn conclusion, statements I, II, and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176049 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241c7bd-4a2b-556f-be9f-8a884addebb9" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "064a647e-d06a-50bb-9cbc-0f17389eca42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295298} {:answer/id #uuid "1066d92d-62c9-5dad-8b4c-dbbe71386d05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295362} {:answer/id #uuid "3f3a0291-6ad9-5566-9fd5-0de08933b4a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295630} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2ff4e9-ee34-51e6-b267-06c09d19013f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296249}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e8ba8e5-5fce-5a75-b9a3-f9b2aab87da3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1618392-44be-533c-81a8-efd84eeee3ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c5a3a079-0ec8-5b33-84d2-568517288149" :question/math-academy-id "q-29608" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=tan⁡x?$ Assume that $n$ represents any integer.\n\n- The function has asymptotes at $x=n\\pi .$\n- $tan⁡(x)=tan⁡(x+2n\\pi ).$\n- The range of the function is $[-1,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=tan⁡x.$\n\n![](images/31/31b430f003171dcc04a8f17402d6035fbbc305c244d3676bbf5da83fa447bd50.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function $y=tan⁡x$ has asymptotes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has period $\\pi .$ So, $tan⁡(x+2n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi +n\\pi )=tan⁡(x+n\\pi )=tan⁡(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The range of $y=tan⁡x$ is $(-\\infty ,+\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297248 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf98ee24-7774-5674-8a1e-63f4f6d225c0" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155da407-c8ba-5fa2-9127-413d6bff8d2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3ln⁡x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295391} {:answer/id #uuid "2ca76ff5-0227-56f5-94be-d80b462a5d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295528} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5899f6-d895-5413-bfee-0587b9cf1149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295802} {:answer/id #uuid "705c2710-b928-5586-a10e-67ac7d7acb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} {:answer/id #uuid "bd595728-5d66-5fce-842a-829a9bf9e495" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{-3}{ln⁡|x|}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "705c2710-b928-5586-a10e-67ac7d7acb16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295884} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6439f4c-9689-5960-aa2c-ca05e0a5ed1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3f7435c-4593-5eb7-979c-b6475dc0ac4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-29831" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-3$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{3}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-3\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-3ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297249 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "df7dddc6-a4fd-5b1b-9b73-8528e5224ca9" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Reciprocal Function and Simplifying the Arbitrary Constant Using the Laws of Logarithms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9f3ccb-57a5-54e7-9651-cd8e38e071bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}ln⁡(5K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295330} {:answer/id #uuid "137963fa-e7bf-5b99-b97d-16a6e2b83b62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295376} {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6cacd1-8561-54df-98e0-e7424109595d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-ln⁡(\\frac{5}{2}K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296168} {:answer/id #uuid "e35e2f64-0c17-5dfe-a27c-81e2452e3e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(|x|+K)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296477}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ba78d99-70e5-5576-9ab3-f6f5d63e5557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c450e82f-bb3f-51ae-bbf9-78580de51634" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eec65b66-7ead-5d6e-b179-5aacd453b9d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-29834" :question/problem "What is the antiderivative of $-\\frac{5}{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $-\\frac{5}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, let's rewrite the constant so that we can combine it with the natural logarithm. For any constant $C,$ we can always write $C=-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K$ where $K$ is some other positive constant. Doing this allows us to combine the constant with $-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|$ term, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -\\frac{5}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡|x|-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡K \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(ln⁡|x|+ln⁡K) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}ln⁡(K|x|).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105716 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c865ec2-5bed-5892-966b-a586097be923" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Sum of a Reciprocal Function and a Power Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abba1e8-07a5-5982-8931-920e2b54ed11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295324} {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} {:answer/id #uuid "218a6eff-a0a2-5775-878d-fb334d854a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295454} {:answer/id #uuid "5203ee4a-c762-565e-a1e7-1c414c5d786d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295738} {:answer/id #uuid "e96a5e23-083b-5d3f-bddc-50fd9a0aed8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296509}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c3a82a-71bb-5b22-a1d7-5d3ffaa433a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4ln⁡|x|+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295384} :answer-field/id #uuid "65380cf5-c8c6-595a-9584-e99d721414af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296843}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "77e80b12-44f5-5ee6-b586-d6d7898a0c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-29915" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1}$ with respect to $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Simplifying the exponents and applying the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\frac{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}{\\sqrt{{x}^{5}}}-5{x}^{-1})\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{5/2-3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4ln⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068860 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9d78ba1c-5297-5540-afdc-d419d33e0cf7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Rational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f24ab63d-b4d8-50ae-8389-5045b51319bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=0\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401963} {:answer/id #uuid "6c1507f7-b1b3-56e3-a38c-1b563d660137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401964} {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401965} {:answer/id #uuid "aa922213-ad7d-513f-ad50-36362f5e8cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401966} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d50863-24e6-52af-928c-18cf82d1862f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401967}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2d7f47d-c746-5735-9e9f-835593e46fab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$pq=-1\\,$ and $\\,{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401965} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f724395-4532-5650-9bbb-1b5a58b225fd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "85741874-6828-5db4-a1ab-e8bb9b524df8" :question/math-academy-id "q-30055" :question/problem "The equation ${x}^{2}-2x+1=1$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^{2}=q.$ Find $\\,pq\\,,$ and the sum of the roots ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side and complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x+1 & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2\\cdot x\\cdot 1+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $p=-1$ and $q=1.$ Thus, $pq=-1.$\n\nWe now solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{(x-1)^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\ |x-1| & =1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ x & =1\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe simplify the two solutions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =1-1=0 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =1+1=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum is ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297279 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0265ab4b-e69c-58cd-bb39-877b73f3298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,11.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295275} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab493a4-b37f-5582-a8b9-4570920c001f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(10.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295861} {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} {:answer/id #uuid "ecdd579a-68d8-530f-aec2-948295be5064" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,5.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296522} {:answer/id #uuid "f541b6c1-d1ab-5c71-9e34-c29b7b48861a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5.5,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296566}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1678182-8bd9-5b40-83a4-560b3c92dfe8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10.5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296126} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0972749-6df3-5836-b156-61b2f017bcaf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "91d38446-45fe-5f55-b13c-726fa96fa8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-30269" :question/problem "For the curve $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={1.5}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=2.5({1.5}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=2.5\\cdot 1=2.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8$ units up, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=2.5+8=10.5.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2.5({1.5}^{0})+8 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 1+8 \\\\  & =2.5+8 \\\\  & =10.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=2.5({1.5}^{x})+8$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,10.5).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087823 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40eeb5a8-3ffe-54d2-b263-f2b1c41fed93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Growth Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e5493f0-49e5-5ac0-a64b-444f26facf5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295811} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf3ffd-b468-57c5-a1fb-5644942d8367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296352} {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} {:answer/id #uuid "e5e90daa-a470-5b92-aeb7-f90fc7f25a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296492} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3169b4-14de-5f3c-837e-5d327959c0c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d228956c-dabb-5adf-b1f0-eea00077c044" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-7)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296375} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ef5eb88-b39d-569b-bc4c-949ed152d723" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296969}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fda2bca-a959-55aa-9acd-07aede112347" :question/math-academy-id "q-30873" :question/problem "For the curve $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$, find the point of intersection with the $y-$axis." :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\n- For the curve $y={10}^{x},$ the point of intersection with the $y$-axis is $(0,1).$\n\n- For the scaled function $y=1.5({10}^{x}),$ the $y$-coordinate becomes\n\n$$\ny=1.5\\cdot 1=1.5.\n$$\n\n- Finally, after a vertical shift of $8.5$ units down, we get a point with the $y$-coordinate\n\n$$\ny=1.5-8.5=-7.\n$$\n\n**Method 2** \n\nTo find the $y$-intercept, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =1.5({10}^{0})-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot 1-8.5 \\\\  & =1.5-8.5 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=1.5({10}^{x})-8.5$ intercepts the $y$-axis at $(0,-7).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5bb394c3-cd7c-523d-88df-b1306d9d528f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c95a3fca-97ff-5e46-ad67-42c5b5b68ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/bc/bc4c0c21c45fc3ed02d88150facf89718896a3e2992105f5a8a5d89b0355f9e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390594} {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} {:answer/id #uuid "e7a74885-a855-53a6-a12b-5c1f9db59688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/06/062274f402396a61c76fae3a47c54de80ad31deac970c746f3f8f4627ce535f1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390596} {:answer/id #uuid "dbeafe1a-8a14-5f3a-8d70-6e398d0ebc6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b3/b3f96cf981a9c127dd0e3c0fa3b73f548db8c640bbc4f0e048b4576356b69532.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390597} {:answer/id #uuid "44eec2a2-c1e5-5830-a6f1-3345cbd3b0c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b48f88ac53107fddece776c81d91fc5684c7d6f06ea3b862fb99cd973eae2dc3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73326b1a-1067-5996-991e-bb9d853a4e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6edb90966ce90a141e3fa4ee1a9eb3cf9c7131e3121e45f56b16bc4395076d5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390595} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5e7cf18-216c-59ac-8255-427849d7152c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6a50d86-9c2e-59fc-b26e-b4043bba0c39" :question/math-academy-id "q-31146" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of  $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4?$" :question/worked-solution "First we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/e2/e2f13711ca62a8a4a698e23bdfe78caca6df44d88ded27b88c517219dffbb866.png)\n\nNext, we shift this graph $4$ units down. \nThe horizontal asymptote under such a transformation becomes $y=-4,$ the $y$-intercept becomes $(0,-3).$\nThe resulting graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{12})}^{x}-4$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/dc/dc7f93220ac18ac5671b7b41de141940ca5296139b836d56755fdccf6164ed26.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064171 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3f9548e6-28ef-5a97-ab04-dfb1b6e23c16" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating the Exponential Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cfff078-9fb4-5b67-bb26-7811957a40b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{ln⁡x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295341} {:answer/id #uuid "36c4e4d8-ea74-510a-8195-fcca114fa7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{{e}^{x}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295573} {:answer/id #uuid "4d71c1ee-dea6-5890-90dd-6cf50a8cd3a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-2x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295711} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3c34e1-3b8b-568c-889c-57fe85dd59ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296256} {:answer/id #uuid "cd13943f-d3e1-5920-9b15-4d7240e4db43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{{e}^{x}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296342}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc3c34e1-3b8b-568c-889c-57fe85dd59ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296256} :answer-field/id #uuid "b94705d1-73b6-521f-a07d-12831457766a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297018}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f474ed40-025d-552e-9e67-da66415ffc42" :question/math-academy-id "q-31245" :question/problem "$\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Factoring out the constant $-2$ and calculating the resulting integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int -2{e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =-2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297342 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064177 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Sum of Exponential Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddda862-700e-5259-8a9b-324825fe885d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{x}+\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295436} {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} {:answer/id #uuid "4aaae1a6-c031-5bc5-a104-23401003f961" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+{x}^{2}+{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295691} {:answer/id #uuid "531024e5-c103-583c-92eb-e4200ac7a6df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3x}-\\frac{{3}^{2x}}{ln⁡x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295744} {:answer/id #uuid "56637843-7c20-5243-80d5-7c1405ee9de3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{2}^{x}}{ln⁡2}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3{e}^{x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3b3aafd2-10f8-5647-aeef-217f0b123766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295606} :answer-field/id #uuid "32505d16-6c49-5e59-9ed0-fdcb1b385a52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "42f6b48b-f5fd-5245-b823-ea76a025e943" :question/math-academy-id "q-31252" :question/problem "$\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Here, we combine the sum rule and our exponential rules to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (2{e}^{x}-{3}^{x}+{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x-\\int {3}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{3}^{x}}{ln⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "617f0fe6-7998-5d77-b11f-55a0eca11440" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Vertically Shifted and Stretched Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84ebbe2d-74ec-5380-9a07-702d026d313d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73590d55299fc519582252723f34bfa143818da7f03a16f7aeb8d208217ee3d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066589} {:answer/id #uuid "00accd83-f7f9-57bb-aa6e-78f38f349bb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b53396e5736d8e09a11acef07594950ee50a0c027e190f0c8eb85880cf721494.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066590} {:answer/id #uuid "0e7e45dc-8e5a-5e38-a11e-07a10f544e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066591} {:answer/id #uuid "319a2339-b438-5f58-b459-e90c8e2d9866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a84109a9d68fe4b185ede5ab47c8ea2c07acd609367b629b594ab0b8e415bc82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066592} {:answer/id #uuid "7941c9a0-4881-5921-a1a9-38e914122b39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e457f65868b58c9246d17744317ad6f76a96c8bd3b024f4fe2d37523a6490fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066593}] :answer-field/id #uuid "375a8c1c-ecfc-5e8e-99de-762874c0a19a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066588} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f871c9fe-ce5c-57e0-b3ab-1c1c8b065a12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b5/b53396e5736d8e09a11acef07594950ee50a0c027e190f0c8eb85880cf721494.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390607} {:answer/id #uuid "3dfaa41c-4be2-5d25-a838-46703edf0110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a8/a84109a9d68fe4b185ede5ab47c8ea2c07acd609367b629b594ab0b8e415bc82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390608} {:answer/id #uuid "857c287c-8899-5fbc-b5f9-5a9ea6523a2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7e/7e457f65868b58c9246d17744317ad6f76a96c8bd3b024f4fe2d37523a6490fa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390609} {:answer/id #uuid "b422f31c-7ac6-593f-9a66-3623920a036a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/73/73590d55299fc519582252723f34bfa143818da7f03a16f7aeb8d208217ee3d4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390610} {:answer/id #uuid "e921280b-88b6-51bc-a473-f6af4ac53ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390611}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e921280b-88b6-51bc-a473-f6af4ac53ca8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390611} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f6f078f-4b80-5f34-98d4-3100f6b4d2cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3af553bc-4342-536e-bf61-1a65d8389d56" :question/math-academy-id "q-31424" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-2?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we plot the graph of $y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}:$\n\n![](images/41/4100e1298857ca36288dc390501fc69dfa68fd13303101d21691d3b056f3365e.png)\n\nNext, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}.$ To do this, we scale the graph above by a factor of $4.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,4)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/37/37bb36e82a659fc52e02f2fe2aa3bdfdc11737af467db62b4694c9b63ee4ae6e.png)\n\nFinally, we plot the graph of $y=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{x}-2.$ To do this, we shift the above graph $2$ units down. The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,4)$ to $(0,2)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=-2.$\n\n![](images/c8/c8652694c97439511e4098e2b8f3d9d1e9727d9953d32348164c3ff7f741f22c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068831 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc402a1d-e538-5510-8a6f-79dd1626ff1f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Shift and Stretch Applied to an Exponential Decay Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1c47f7-5b8c-5177-997f-9c9709da23dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295599} {:answer/id #uuid "4e3cb064-2eaf-5f92-9194-de89c9125599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295717} {:answer/id #uuid "7b75ed7d-abd8-526c-8c58-6405c2b625a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295942} {:answer/id #uuid "d96c6138-85f4-575a-bbd3-c78b6c063670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296413} {:answer/id #uuid "f51945cd-cb43-5802-a043-9d995443a3f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a1c47f7-5b8c-5177-997f-9c9709da23dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295599} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9d0e6b0-fcd1-5675-b95f-02f242a5a3ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b52cd54-7f7c-57a1-892c-73badca0d5f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-31435" :question/problem "![](images/01/016286e82482af83783f0eef6361591861b564643d4d9f010f2b82999fcaad27.png)\n\nThe graph above shows $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c.$ Find the value of $k-c.$" :question/worked-solution "The horizontal asymptote of the curve $y=k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c$ is $y=c.$ In the graph above, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-4.$ Hence, $c=-4.$\n\nWe can solve for $k$ by finding a point on the graph and substituting for $x$ and $y.$ The labeled point on the graph is $(0,-2),$ so we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-2$ into the function and solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}+c & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4 & =y \\\\ k{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{0}-4 & =-2 \\\\ k-4 & =-2 \\\\ k & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nk-c=2-(-4)=6.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the curve is $y=2{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{x}-4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390645 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f61a7c3-82e6-5f90-8a95-4a79078fd542" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Growth Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a771d975-2aaa-513d-bcb8-8905d9edc2ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/5559cd21367ed24058d7f0a3f1f646feb6cbdc269cbd4128788b17cbd331a01e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390647} {:answer/id #uuid "41d7c55e-75b0-54d8-907f-70f90a5594aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b0/b05cb7e1d42380d1a1ac76139b39bbed49f429dbc43fddbae79b47e61d5659fd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390648} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd1f9e6-af1e-5909-94b9-5f25d90a06ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30b885e19455654b28e87aba56427a82bfb9abe98d28dc5b542214a7fd55315f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390649} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4ddff0-6b9c-5567-88cf-552367429984" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/7f/7fbb4a84bc3fa077711d003c7b05fa8bccfb84b31a112736363fa4de00315e54.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390650} {:answer/id #uuid "b0ef6180-669c-546a-a8a2-ed7f728e356b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/78/7868ed246bfad0cbcb848098c39443616acf8554620537ad5cf53fa46fdaf9e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390651}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dd1f9e6-af1e-5909-94b9-5f25d90a06ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/30b885e19455654b28e87aba56427a82bfb9abe98d28dc5b542214a7fd55315f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390649} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9df4524-7dfb-5370-9bcc-d5a8bffd76f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d6b5ac66-3c43-5e20-bd0d-692b07d708ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-317281" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{3}^{x}+1?$" :question/worked-solution "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={3}^{x}.$\n\n![](images/17/17a76468bd4f446f420d784ae479724135b2af3369a7d69f87c67507e74a8901.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{3}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n![](images/ac/ac5e769c069b753bbf3f6a9a276b69881a3d2699afc4f62498233ecc526f5a2b.png)\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $1$ unit up to give $y=-{3}^{x}+1.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,0)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=1.$\n\n![](images/c6/c69c8be5828709cef483cba56d5842b973cf718e7592a8fba9e825be08204f5b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0f61a7c3-82e6-5f90-8a95-4a79078fd542" :knowledge-point/title "Vertical Reflections of Exponential Growth Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6201916-ff41-54d1-97cf-62c6516a067d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f95a21987377cae131f3d627ae1dc5156f4871a73cae4fec667521f3e66fcf7c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390654} {:answer/id #uuid "5e48d6e0-ded7-563c-8901-966cfafd124e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/46/469aaa6b3110693e02dfd987c104169c603a73c69782ec491700ea7d250103a7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390655} {:answer/id #uuid "70cf48de-781d-5c0e-b225-b89c2b1f1b54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c31f62568e601154d960de5d0a046ad9e1aeb4fa5eafefcd82abb4205797c77.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390656} {:answer/id #uuid "f38fc2a2-80f0-5cf9-a37f-f2e0720c822a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/22/227aa9ad8e6d95c99e3984ce9959de38d7ca50c98111e5ba94c0a8036a7a5f8e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186390657} {:answer/id #uuid "00d16dba-c