th Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f420e0-2ce0-5aca-8dee-2fc02f4be838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297656} {:answer/id #uuid "2decc769-5d34-52a4-8f61-0d917cf2c0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{5}+{y}^{2})(x-{y}^{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297746} {:answer/id #uuid "84d3cf7b-8bb1-5c05-bad0-4556bf860b32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298125} {:answer/id #uuid "903c4fc8-2bfb-5749-a8dc-9a4f1681fb2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})({x}^{3}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298178} {:answer/id #uuid "bd0ab2a9-e83d-55df-8e55-44c8b207e2a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17f420e0-2ce0-5aca-8dee-2fc02f4be838" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297656} :answer-field/id #uuid "710ade4f-6788-57df-bfae-27eab8eaa863" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186299022}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a03ef93a-a766-5bc2-9cd5-d8ee9f70bca4" :question/math-academy-id "q-31991" :question/problem "Factor ${x}^{4}-{y}^{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{x}^{4}-{y}^{6}={({x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{3})}^{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({x}^{2})}^{2}-{({y}^{3})}^{2}=({x}^{2}+{y}^{3})({x}^{2}-{y}^{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069268 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a7f04ab7-f6f6-5d01-bfcf-b667e37e4f0b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation When the Solutions Are Irrational"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "824db7a0-468b-5b7c-bdd3-7bd19185dd56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066779} {:answer/id #uuid "f44af2a5-1591-5f5e-93ba-40c0ddd584b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z =-8 ± 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066780} {:answer/id #uuid "30dfca08-4199-59da-8d02-2600e0f2122b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 75, z = 5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066781} {:answer/id #uuid "4a3b9749-a33c-5c57-89ac-26939f855dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q = 80, z = 5 ± 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066782} {:answer/id #uuid "6d135e5e-ebb4-5c27-84bf-0be68ee6c029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p + q =-70, z = ± \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066783}] :answer-field/id #uuid "988361f0-82f8-5fa0-bce8-8775fead2e27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186066778} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} {:answer/id #uuid "23289d14-87e9-57fb-bbda-cdd305eb9ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=-70,\\,z=\\pm \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402358} {:answer/id #uuid "691a3cdd-d3d5-50e7-a708-646cfa31e0a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-8\\pm 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402359} {:answer/id #uuid "823c0a9e-ef4b-5a9d-a5eb-0fe274767e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=75,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402360} {:answer/id #uuid "ee362d99-5b0e-55c5-83d5-a9a8872163ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9eb9e11c-6e9c-5d9f-85b6-41a705579cde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p+q=80,\\,z=-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402357} :answer-field/id #uuid "0174c191-2a23-5d0f-b8d7-9880c6f7ee41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402356}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c0076f5-08a5-5d4c-9789-196650ab8295" :question/math-academy-id "q-3435" :question/problem "The equation ${z}^{2}-50=-10z$ can be written in the form $(z+p{)}^{2}=q.$ Find $p+q,$ and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $-10z$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}-50 & =-10z \\\\ {z}^{2}+10z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z & =50 \\\\ {z}^{2}+2\\cdot 5\\cdot z+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2}-{5}^{2} & =50 \\\\ (z+5{)}^{2} & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=5,q=75,$ and $p+q=80.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(z+5{)}^{2} & =75 \\\\ \\sqrt{(z+5{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ |z+5| & =\\sqrt{75} \\\\ z+5 & =\\pm 5\\sqrt{3} \\\\ z & =-5\\pm 5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15ff9d00-cfe3-5000-9f39-f5b084d00080" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319455} {:answer/id #uuid "9035921e-6fcf-5772-b9a5-7d9a496b6106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319823} {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc970a1-4ea5-589f-8352-ce90a07452c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320063} {:answer/id #uuid "f9a74ebb-b1db-5cf0-a20e-5165336efc1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320137}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d6df4228-d351-594e-9eea-e865f3a883bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320046} :answer-field/id #uuid "1cb04ca6-e219-5d5e-ab67-487f7d66d988" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c7529a5-2ffe-53f0-9bca-cd9b5d97a802" :question/math-academy-id "q-35192" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b9b836c-fae2-50e0-a795-3a27cfdd5785" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319421} {:answer/id #uuid "3492000c-c2fa-51a4-bcee-24c414d9ca52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=7x-a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319554} {:answer/id #uuid "8d3d6dcf-2ec9-5287-a8ef-e92f9f472281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319812} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} {:answer/id #uuid "d00e46ee-e183-5993-a320-dcbaa9aa4eae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x-a}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccb04d9c-7b29-5181-b95e-06fc65228ede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320017} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5d4e30f-32ac-5460-b456-38e5a87775f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "abfc488e-a974-5dbc-b40c-2f281295336b" :question/math-academy-id "q-35217" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=ax-7$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=ax-7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=ay-7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =ay-7 \\\\ x+7 & =ay \\\\ \\frac{x+7}{a} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{x+7}{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} {:answer/id #uuid "574ba857-9f96-590d-abdc-bb096d802847" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319648} {:answer/id #uuid "ac7a7fe3-5133-5ba3-baf2-2c6b2e00ce1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319916} {:answer/id #uuid "e1c8a647-b5b3-5af4-85b7-661bdc068c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320074} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b296d9-adc9-527b-a4b4-ccaabe649e19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ccc7bce-8234-5ee1-bd8b-2bcdd3fcbd07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319574} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e14ff5e-6a4f-5e24-8876-c8a24ddf7181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ffa1d84-420d-51e5-b5d5-2102976566da" :question/math-academy-id "q-35243" :question/problem "![](images/67/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078876 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078416 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "40a70c8d-dc8e-52bd-a679-ffd6d1940451" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Function With a Parameter"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} {:answer/id #uuid "8ee8eb52-9b9f-56f1-9ecb-13190d21a579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319819} {:answer/id #uuid "b056fbea-f31f-565e-a6ee-a22a4a856c27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319927} {:answer/id #uuid "b321e6e4-fc1e-54d2-9a1a-305a18b80a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319939} {:answer/id #uuid "b495768c-d594-5351-a058-aad82b451854" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=x+11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28a8e3f3-e6e5-59bd-9144-f9733223829f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319519} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5fa981-8f9f-5e72-b3b9-955186ce41e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320236}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40530ca3-e238-5640-94b0-1433afa01d34" :question/math-academy-id "q-35310" :question/problem "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{a}+11$, where $a$ is a non-zero constant." :question/worked-solution "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{x}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{y}{a}+11\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{y}{a}+11 \\\\ x-11 & =\\frac{y}{a} \\\\ a(x-11) & =y \\\\ ax-11a & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=ax-11a\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185960eb-bfc4-5b04-9b8e-889e9fa563fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402404} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} {:answer/id #uuid "de3fec5a-67b7-579c-a809-55a0c1f4c2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402406} {:answer/id #uuid "10adf48f-3c4e-533f-ba78-f17c436ead07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402407} {:answer/id #uuid "8d0475d5-57f8-5642-b3fc-c1fb1233e0f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402408}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7e16aa5-8900-58f3-8772-5eddc1069f2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402405} :answer-field/id #uuid "181f4699-3530-5c7f-a1cf-43e09f53ac45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402403}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "da57a6bc-d0db-59f4-81d7-c54fef1172ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-35379" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3).$" :question/worked-solution "We identify the leading term and compute its limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}+3) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{4}) \\\\  & =-\\,(\\infty ) \\\\  & =-\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323737 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7739ff43-c68c-58ca-8bbe-3cf898ab85f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322081} {:answer/id #uuid "8d033687-0dda-5d24-a50d-81609e131df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322273} {:answer/id #uuid "8f052679-93df-563a-8c3a-49901beaf19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322283} {:answer/id #uuid "cc2a112f-a59c-5195-8734-7545ee263058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322736} {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eefd406f-e3a9-51c3-9df4-f6d2a447cae1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323031} :answer-field/id #uuid "661e0d23-dac0-58fe-aeb6-82bf0e4b60b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323373}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "544516cf-cdec-5de8-af07-1ff7e5be101e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36004" :question/problem "![](images/15/1597ad53471d0d9b44812c5caa31bcb160cfa150d1df9a9314447fa49e62ccff.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in [-6,0]$\n- $x\\in [-6,4)$\n- $x\\in [4,10)$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in [-6,0]$ and $x\\in [4,10).$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/7c/7cb282e3fa66af801ff03816973d5f5f1d0cb41e39d08a8addd27d7aa9c5b9df.png)\n\n      \n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the interval $[-6,4]$  because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/00/000953609a7b3e13ad09473ead62f96aa895c7f94aa618884e69883595004fd4.png)\n\n \n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I  and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "014f6668-666f-5040-a144-715105254126" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321160} {:answer/id #uuid "26c34079-b0a6-5fc3-9a77-ee93f052ff20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321452} {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} {:answer/id #uuid "968df320-cd19-54ac-b971-d412835fd3ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322348} {:answer/id #uuid "f59bf802-b4bc-54f8-8889-7338410f2cd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80353f56-f611-551a-b4e9-abf19a92d1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322153} :answer-field/id #uuid "28b86894-2e1c-5167-ac3e-a4dd5b6514f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323253}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d31db013-e3f7-5fe2-aefa-8428da418673" :question/math-academy-id "q-36011" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b02c2e94e144935f0ff96846cd51b5a969fa8f884a7ca1d09abb231b7c945232.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible? \n\n- $x\\in (-4,-2)$\n- $x\\in [-2,3]$\n- $x\\in [-3,3]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.  \n\n- The given function is invertible on each of the intervals $x\\in (-4,-2)$ and $x\\in [-2,3].$ Indeed, any horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/53/53fe88780b2ccb7522d2f1baa711162ec99300c2a2d4da29bba9ca532026cd07.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on  the interval $x\\in [-3,3]$  because some lines will  intersect the curve at more than one point.\n\n![](images/63/63cee4fc05ab17a8e69d9bfae578fa83c24d73e4677065a54a33a25e0871e718.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125021 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124473 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffd143c8-a993-5d0b-85a5-821d6482ae60" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intervals on Which a Function is Invertible Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4caea11f-484f-50a4-a520-52df284329ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321753} {:answer/id #uuid "54e75cb9-e5b0-5ea8-9eaf-304e5de715df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321816} {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} {:answer/id #uuid "c304d552-6f05-5067-a107-0c7b855a1c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322661} {:answer/id #uuid "f99f1c2f-bc18-5796-a3e2-28f9f9b5b60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323112}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "888f7548-fc8c-5732-aae9-9e3aa70555ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322236} :answer-field/id #uuid "9392c5de-5972-51d1-8356-6d7fe3ba1f53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e891cfb3-f978-5b44-b8bf-4d4f9f71b503" :question/math-academy-id "q-36941" :question/problem "![](images/3c/3cbdac583a4cdf1b5eb761892934516a0b4edd1487bd402ae0014859c4a0ad53.png)\n\nOn which of the following intervals is the function $f(x)$ (shown above) invertible?\n\n- $x\\in (-2,0]$\n- $x\\in (-2,2]$\n- $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$" :question/worked-solution "A function is invertible on a particular interval if and only if it's a one-to-one function on that interval. To determine the intervals on which the function is invertible, we use the horizontal line test.\n\n- The given function is invertible on the interval $x\\in (-2,0].$ Indeed, a horizontal line will intersect the curve at most once, no matter where we draw the line.\n\n![](images/d7/d7c6e8d314483ad67e89ce5d4675b7af27fa08c88a4ed9d95eed9ad2a4da51cc.png)\n\n- The given function is **not** invertible on the intervals $x\\in (-2,2]$ and $x\\in [-4,-2]\\cup [2,4]$ because some horizontal lines will intersect the curve more than once.\n\n![](images/ce/cee829f7e3d5f32b0acb54cb5c40406d8391d2a2967c68df0c1f5e1cae87d729.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323957 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c73db44-2ec2-5025-9431-acb02e414916" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Trigonometric Ratio Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8126b5-2054-5d88-865f-be6cbe674d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321368} {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} {:answer/id #uuid "51eee8c3-23ea-5613-a452-4c2182222cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321794} {:answer/id #uuid "5ba4b8d7-052a-5cf2-86db-50636fe22d83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321871} {:answer/id #uuid "c2131ada-4ea2-5f29-85f1-aa42dc442a53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3466518b-1130-594a-9f91-8439ec16fa46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321544} :answer-field/id #uuid "617b107f-dc16-5899-aab5-ab0f5850cc8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9112180a-ac6b-5472-ba8b-819d346ead1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-37915" :question/problem "![](images/6c/6c36dbce847c719accc77fa15f33dfb9a87cfae669691afe30f7693663076d2e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4},$ use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/52/52fe94bb303a001e89f9db221e9730d4ca8c5c284217aa20fb6084ee041dec66.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos⁡\\theta ,\\,y=sin⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =2-2{(\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =2-2(\\frac{10}{16}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{20}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our final answer is $2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{4}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402649 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09148cf6-e660-5422-9fa7-a9f4738defd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402651} {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} {:answer/id #uuid "f4481476-873e-5b4e-a202-210e8449c587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402653} {:answer/id #uuid "63b1b59c-8954-5973-ae03-cc16a86d080d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402654} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f0241d-3539-5ced-b138-fdde689dc4a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3340c634-8f83-5193-ae75-2b37a7b6c946" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402652} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f13134e-6c13-54bc-aaf2-8a968a13df74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4ced118-ede6-56c5-a59a-002b28515d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-38580" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea87f040e4249a04c21dd6c8466ffd194d60753056c29f00d8956e182db61ff3.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=F(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The degree of $F(x)$ is odd.\nII. The degree of $F(x)$ is even.\nIII. The coefficient of the leading term is positive." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $F(x)$ is\n\n- $F(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ and\n\n- $F(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $F(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $F(x)$ is positive.\n\nSo II is false, and I and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "520ce037-6d65-5a87-816c-38b9a9d22f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324642} {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ee0489-97b3-566f-8f0e-c62d506da27a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325704} {:answer/id #uuid "cb11e15c-7b44-5232-8e56-3e85080ab86c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325731} {:answer/id #uuid "e61483f3-87ce-5ef0-b035-b6a704d798b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b137372b-d54b-51cb-8c70-5c8eaabe8e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325482} :answer-field/id #uuid "ceb549ca-f772-54ab-ac92-58fb214b0833" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326598}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f40578d-2e88-5fde-bb73-48381fd621fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-38617" :question/problem "![](images/fb/fb4959f437e11b767be136a2a024260d45ed29805cd57b1832d74d7cc2da11c1.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n- The degree of $P(x)$ is odd.\n- The coefficient of the leading term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, statements I and II are false, while statement III is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129680 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fbb6282-911b-5b66-b192-305d440d6e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = 2x^{6} + x^{4} - 5x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127562} {:answer/id #uuid "a5a044cc-7963-5872-b7e6-6d9a2f224fbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-7x^{8} + 6x^{7} - 4x^{3} + 2x^{2} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127563} {:answer/id #uuid "ecb2454f-bf3e-5aa4-afce-9896ef787f3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-5x^{5} + 3x^{2} + x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127564} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5b9971-58f5-5d6d-9f95-7c155519b2d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y =-x^{3} + 2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127565} {:answer/id #uuid "4c0c079c-6769-57c1-b736-a89c4eef2463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = x^{3} - x^{2} + x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127566}] :answer-field/id #uuid "db6524ac-b4e5-521e-a137-1133e610aa7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127561} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} {:answer/id #uuid "0cf46694-f517-57c1-b656-f30bd243fe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-7{x}^{8}+6{x}^{7}-4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402658} {:answer/id #uuid "0cabba40-fb5e-5284-8447-f1cf64e6820e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{6}+{x}^{4}-5x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402659} {:answer/id #uuid "e52d814a-f821-5e6e-934c-6f5a0f7ba906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402660} {:answer/id #uuid "343e1b78-b8cf-5288-841e-bb541f7dedd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-5{x}^{5}+3{x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb2ff6c4-35ee-5c86-88b6-f2b778bb9b49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402657} :answer-field/id #uuid "702788a0-aadc-5877-8e1c-f586a9038e3d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186402656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e1b36cf-56ef-56e7-80a1-3a4e3d34ad42" :question/math-academy-id "q-38624" :question/problem "![](images/d8/d873bd2af8e397d6c540267340b6105f413928c4279b939110aa4bb5b00d8948.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is negative, which means that $P(0)<0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, only $P(x)=-{x}^{3}+2x-1$ satisties all these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326703 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11ec8bba-61a9-5e53-9f83-ae6306a35d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}{x}^{6}+\\sqrt{7}{x}^{3}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324133} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} {:answer/id #uuid "4a6cc728-1b58-586c-b553-5f4c6de4cade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3{x}^{3}+8x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324575} {:answer/id #uuid "858b3ba7-0126-52d6-a92c-623111bcf464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{x}^{8}+4{x}^{2}+6x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325099} {:answer/id #uuid "d96579c4-211b-512e-819d-f2e5755afaf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6{x}^{5}+4{x}^{4}-7{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d3e9e3c-8f63-5b55-a2b5-d6ae2f7cc85b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324219} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c01c0f4-68e1-5f41-ac69-b50a572974f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c04700c1-06e0-50a6-8674-83b9d2f36915" :question/math-academy-id "q-38626" :question/problem "![](images/eb/eb467eed4327c7feaa4cb0aad4495032fe52518891bb6d33227352c953ce4d35.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is positive, which means that $P(0)>0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=-2{x}^{4}+{x}^{2}-x+2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129154 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a66188f6-3228-59e9-9766-0f9bacbe8443" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Given Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42f76b4e-c2e3-5210-b8b9-db6bc53bafc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324510} {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} {:answer/id #uuid "aefcef1a-2486-5ad4-889b-d4796ad4702f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325458} {:answer/id #uuid "dfae7751-a48a-56fb-8fe9-dabe433a0205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325924} {:answer/id #uuid "fd71fe36-ab9d-5173-8340-e63507c5a1eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "868d64bf-05a4-5a8a-bb2f-f215d1a3fb4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325114} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1d289fa-530e-56e8-81da-88ee4282c3b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326541}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbe22a70-053f-5354-8d7b-dc32dacb2db4" :question/math-academy-id "q-38770" :question/problem "![](images/cd/cd19717bd41b787346a96394ce3bc4e38fe690340bb73bd3d11139d41ff977e2.png)\n\nThe graph of a polynomial $p(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $x=2$ is a simple root.\n- $x=-1$ is a root of even multiplicity.\n- $p(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The root $x=2$ is a simple root because the graph crosses the $x$-axis at the point and the curve is not tangent to the $x$-axis at that point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The root $x=-1$  has an even multiplicity because at $x=-1$ the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction.\n\n- Statement III is false. As $x$ moves right in the positive direction towards $+\\infty ,$ the $y$ value moves up in the positive direction towards $+\\infty .$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326735 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129302 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "503a2d0a-ba6c-5529-9f26-b83c769a26a2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Domain of a Transformed Reciprocal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b52373-27a9-501e-8947-0c1f244a213e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324149} {:answer/id #uuid "5bec4237-30c8-54eb-a9f5-13ccaf205551" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324732} {:answer/id #uuid "85ed53c0-2287-5169-b4e2-c59609989228" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325105} {:answer/id #uuid "d964678d-9ceb-5d76-929a-65b4dd6a6052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (2,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325861} {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df8ff16b-fe1f-5c14-b271-df5bace274b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325923} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9cd61a3-c73b-5511-815b-f6d529fa4ad2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326616}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "45b870b0-b774-5b07-bef2-72f771e77f18" :question/math-academy-id "q-38999" :question/problem "What is the domain of the function $y=\\frac{2}{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values of $x$ that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the domain is $x\\in (-\\infty ,0)\\cup (0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083681 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083310 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "001262b8-82b7-5421-80ac-8608b20d4be1" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Cubic Polynomial Using the Rational Roots Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "135f42a4-d1ee-5f2c-af3a-0c8aa0176bf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324143} {:answer/id #uuid "268df909-c8fb-563f-af2f-c08134584f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324283} {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} {:answer/id #uuid "8b3c6363-b72d-5a0d-9eec-918fa9888a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325152} {:answer/id #uuid "fc9d3f67-6d9d-5067-8456-4fe529f4d545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69e770f2-5fac-5581-b92a-b1b948231e35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324857} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f2953d1-fb0d-5e3e-8ec9-21bd1672c52d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326351}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f572c08e-edf8-5f9d-9827-c106bc98ece4" :question/math-academy-id "q-39033" :question/problem "Given that the polynomial $f(x)=3{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}-15x-25$ has one of its roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ find the sum of the distinct real roots of $f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a polynomial with integer coefficients, the rational roots theorem states that any rational roots of the polynomial must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of the constant term}}{\\text{factor of the leading coefficient}}.\n$$\n\nFor the given polynomial $f(x),$ the constant term is $-25$ and the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, any rational roots of $f(x)$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\pm \\frac{\\text{factor of }-25}{\\text{factor of }3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the possible rational roots are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 5,\\,\\pm 25,\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{5}{3},\\,\\pm \\frac{25}{3}\n$$\n\nOf these roots, the only roots in the interval $(-2,-\\frac{1}{2}),$ are $-1$ and $-\\frac{5}{3}.$\n\nNow, let's use synthetic division to test whether $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is a factor of $f(x).$ We get the following:\n\n|  | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-\\frac{5}{3}$ | $3$ | $5$ | $-15$ | $-25$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-5$ | $0$ | $25$ |\n|  | $3$ | $0$ | $-15$ | $0$ |\n\nSince the remainder is $0,$ we conclude that $(x+\\frac{5}{3})$ is indeed a factor of $f(x).$ Moreover, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15).\n$$\n\nSince the highest-degree factor is now a quadratic, we can continue factoring using the usual methods:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+\\frac{5}{3})(3{x}^{2}-15) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})({x}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =3(x+\\frac{5}{3})(x+\\sqrt{5})(x-\\sqrt{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to $0,$ we find the roots $x=-\\frac{5}{3},-\\sqrt{5},\\sqrt{5}.$ Summing these roots, we get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{5}{3}-\\sqrt{5}+\\sqrt{5}=-\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}]}
